window.SPEAKING_TASKS = [{"id": 1, "task_number": 1, "task_key": "giving_advice", "task_title": "Giving Advice", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Your coworker Daniel tells you he was just offered a promotion to team lead, but it would mean managing three people who used to be his equals, including one close friend. He is worried the friendship will suffer and that the others might resent him. He asks for your advice on whether he should accept the promotion and how to handle the transition. What would you tell him?", "model_answer": "Daniel, first of all, congratulations, because being offered this role means your manager already trusts your judgment. My honest advice is to take it, but go in with a clear plan for how you'll handle the relationships. I'd sit down with your friend privately before anything is announced, and be upfront that the friendship matters to you and that you don't want the new title to change how you treat each other outside work. At the same time, be honest that during work hours you'll need to treat everyone fairly and consistently, including him, so nobody can accuse you of favouritism. For the wider team, I'd hold a short meeting in your first week to explain your approach, ask what support they need from you, and make it clear their input is welcome. Resentment usually grows when people feel decisions are being made behind their backs, so keep communication open and check in regularly, not just when something goes wrong. It will feel awkward for a few weeks, that's normal, but if you lead with respect instead of authority, most people adjust quickly. Don't let the fear of one uncomfortable conversation stop you from taking a real career opportunity.", "tips": "Structure your answer as: acknowledge the opportunity, give a clear yes/no recommendation, then two or three concrete steps. Use direct address like 'Daniel, I'd suggest...' to sound natural and personal rather than like a list.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 2, "task_number": 1, "task_key": "giving_advice", "task_title": "Giving Advice", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Your neighbour Priya recently adopted a rescue dog that barks constantly whenever she leaves the apartment, and other tenants have started complaining to the building manager. Priya loves the dog and doesn't want to give it up, but she's worried about being evicted. She asks you for advice on what she should do. What would you tell her?", "model_answer": "Priya, I completely understand not wanting to give the dog up, especially after everything he's already been through, so let's think about this practically. The barking is almost always separation anxiety, which is very common in rescue dogs who aren't used to being alone yet, and the good news is it's usually fixable with patience. I'd start by talking to the building manager directly, before things escalate further, and explain that you're actively working on a solution. That alone often buys you goodwill and more time. Then I'd suggest gradually training him to be comfortable alone, leaving for just a few minutes at first and slowly increasing the time, so he learns that you always come back. A puzzle toy or a piece of your worn clothing left with him can also help calm him down while you're gone. If the barking continues, look into a dog walker or doggy daycare for a few days a week so he isn't isolated for long stretches. Some buildings also allow a white noise machine or calming music to mask outside sounds that trigger him. It'll take a couple of weeks of consistency, but most dogs do improve significantly once they trust the routine.", "tips": "Show empathy first before problem-solving, since the friend is emotionally attached to the dog. Offer a mix of an immediate step (talk to the manager) and a longer-term behavioural fix, and mention a realistic timeline to sound credible.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 3, "task_number": 1, "task_key": "giving_advice", "task_title": "Giving Advice", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Your cousin Marcus just moved to a new city for a job and has been feeling isolated because he doesn't know anyone there yet. He works from home most days and says he sometimes goes several days without speaking to another person in real life. He calls you asking how he can meet people and start building a social life. What would you tell him?", "model_answer": "Marcus, that sounds really tough, especially working from home where you don't even get the small daily interactions an office gives you naturally. My first suggestion would be to find something with a regular schedule, because consistency is what actually builds friendships, not one-off events. Look into a recreational sports league, a climbing gym, or a weekly hobby class like pottery or cooking, something you'd genuinely enjoy so you keep showing up even on days you don't feel social. I'd also recommend using an app like Meetup to find interest-based groups in your new city, since it's specifically designed to connect people who just relocated. Another idea is to work from a coffee shop or a coworking space a couple of days a week instead of always staying home, just to be around other people and break up the isolation. If your new job has any social events, even small ones like a team lunch, make the effort to go, because those low-pressure settings can turn into real friendships over time. Give it about two or three months of consistent effort before judging whether it's working, because building a social circle from scratch always takes longer than people expect.", "tips": "Emphasize consistency and routine as the key theme, since that is what genuinely builds friendships. Give at least three distinct types of suggestions (a hobby, an app, a workspace change) to show range of vocabulary and ideas.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 4, "task_number": 1, "task_key": "giving_advice", "task_title": "Giving Advice", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Your friend Alina has been saving for a down payment on a condo, but her sister just asked to borrow a large sum of money to cover an unexpected medical bill. Alina wants to help her sister but is afraid it will delay her own plans to buy a home for years. She asks you what she should do. What advice would you give her?", "model_answer": "Alina, this is a genuinely hard situation because both things matter, your sister's health and your own future, so I don't think there's one perfect answer, but here's how I'd approach it. First, find out exactly how much your sister actually needs and whether there are other options available to her, like a payment plan with the hospital, a personal loan, or provincial health coverage she might not have explored yet. Sometimes the full amount isn't actually necessary if those alternatives are on the table. If you do decide to help, I'd suggest lending only what you can afford without completely derailing your own savings, and treating it as a real loan with a written agreement and a repayment timeline, even between family. That protects the relationship if things get complicated later. You could also offer a smaller amount as a gift and be upfront that it's all you can give right now, rather than stretching yourself thin trying to cover everything. Whatever you choose, talk to your sister honestly about your own financial goals so she understands the sacrifice involved and doesn't come back expecting more later. Helping family is important, but it shouldn't come at the cost of your own stability for years to come.", "tips": "This prompt has real tension between two values, so acknowledge that difficulty explicitly rather than picking a side too fast. Offer a middle-ground solution (partial help, a written agreement) to show nuanced thinking.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 5, "task_number": 1, "task_key": "giving_advice", "task_title": "Giving Advice", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Your friend Jordan has been offered a fully remote job with a significant pay increase, but it would mean leaving a workplace he loves, with colleagues he's close to and a manager who has mentored him for years. He keeps going back and forth and asks for your opinion on what he should do. What advice would you give him?", "model_answer": "Jordan, I think the fact that you keep going back and forth tells me this decision is closer than it might first appear, so let's break it into pieces instead of trying to decide all at once. Money matters, but it shouldn't be the only factor, especially since you've said this mentor relationship has genuinely shaped your career. I'd ask yourself whether you could get a similar boost, maybe not identical, by negotiating a raise or a title change where you currently are, since many employers will counter an offer rather than lose a strong employee. If that's not realistic, think about whether the relationships you value could survive the move in some form, through occasional calls or meetups, or whether they're really tied to being in that specific office every day. I'd also weigh how much you personally value remote flexibility long term, because that lifestyle change can be significant even beyond the paycheque. There's no wrong choice here, but I'd lean toward at least having an honest conversation with your current manager before deciding, since you might be surprised by what they're willing to offer to keep you. Whatever you choose, make sure it's based on what you actually want in five years, not just what feels safest right now.", "tips": "This is a career trade-off prompt, so structure the advice around weighing competing factors (money, mentorship, flexibility) rather than giving a single flat recommendation. End with a forward-looking suggestion like negotiating first.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 6, "task_number": 1, "task_key": "giving_advice", "task_title": "Giving Advice", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Your younger brother Tyler just started college and tells you he's failing one of his courses because he's been spending most nights playing online games with his roommates instead of studying. He knows it's a problem but says he doesn't know how to stop without losing his social life. He asks for your advice. What would you tell him?", "model_answer": "Tyler, I appreciate that you're being honest about this instead of just ignoring it, so let's figure out a plan that doesn't force you to choose between friends and grades. The real issue usually isn't the gaming itself, it's not having any structure around when it happens. I'd suggest setting specific study blocks earlier in the evening, say from six to nine, where your phone and console are completely off limits, and treating that time as non-negotiable, like a class you can't skip. Once that block is done, you've genuinely earned the rest of the night to game guilt-free, which actually makes it more enjoyable anyway. You could also talk to your roommates about this, because if they know your schedule, they'll likely respect it and might even join you in studying first before playing together. Another option is studying in the library or a common area instead of your room, just to physically separate yourself from the temptation. Check in with your professor about the failing course too, since many will let you make up missed work if you show you're taking it seriously now. The key is starting this new routine immediately, because the longer you wait, the harder the course will be to recover.", "tips": "Reframe the problem away from 'stop gaming' toward 'add structure,' since that is more realistic and actionable advice a real person would give. Mention talking to the professor as a concrete recovery step, not just a prevention step.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 7, "task_number": 1, "task_key": "giving_advice", "task_title": "Giving Advice", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Your friend Sam runs a small home bakery business and just found out that a much bigger bakery is opening two blocks away next month. Sam is worried about losing customers and doesn't have much money for advertising. Sam calls you for advice on how to compete with the new bakery. What would you tell Sam?", "model_answer": "Sam, I know this news is stressful, but a small bakery has real advantages a big chain simply can't copy, so let's focus on those instead of trying to out-advertise them. First, lean into what makes your bakery personal, things like remembering regular customers' orders, offering custom cakes for special occasions, or featuring recipes tied to your own background, since people genuinely value that kind of connection. I'd also suggest building a strong presence on social media, which costs almost nothing, by posting photos of daily specials, behind-the-scenes baking videos, and customer stories, since that kind of content spreads through shares far more than paid ads do. Partnering with nearby cafes or offices to supply pastries for their events is another low-cost way to bring in steady income without competing directly on foot traffic. You could also introduce a simple loyalty program, like a free item after ten purchases, to keep your regulars coming back instead of trying the new place out of curiosity. Finally, don't be afraid to ask your existing customers directly what would make them keep choosing you, because they'll often tell you exactly what to focus on. Staying small and personal is actually your strongest advantage here, not a weakness.", "tips": "Frame the small business as having genuine competitive advantages rather than only disadvantages, since this makes the advice more original and specific to the scenario. Suggest at least one no-cost or low-cost idea since the friend explicitly says money is tight.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 8, "task_number": 1, "task_key": "giving_advice", "task_title": "Giving Advice", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Your friend Hana has been renting the same apartment for six years and just learned her landlord is raising the rent by 20 percent, which she can barely afford. She likes the neighbourhood and doesn't want to move, but isn't sure whether she should try to negotiate, look for somewhere new, or just accept the increase. She asks for your advice. What would you tell her?", "model_answer": "Hana, a 20 percent increase is a big jump, so I definitely wouldn't just accept it without at least trying to push back first. Since you've been a reliable tenant for six years, that's actually leverage, because landlords lose money finding and vetting new renters, dealing with a vacant unit, and repainting or repairing between tenants. I'd suggest writing a polite but direct email to your landlord pointing out your long history of on-time payments and asking if there's room to negotiate a smaller increase, maybe five or ten percent instead. It also helps to research what similar units in the neighbourhood are actually renting for right now, so you can show the number you're asking for is reasonable, not just convenient for you. If your landlord won't budge at all, then it's worth spending a weekend looking at what else is available nearby, even just to know your real options and strengthen your negotiating position. Sometimes simply mentioning that you're looking around is enough to make a landlord reconsider. If you do end up needing to move, try to give yourself a few months of lead time so you're not rushed into a worse deal out of desperation. Either way, don't assume the number they gave you is final before you've at least asked.", "tips": "Emphasize the tenant's negotiating leverage (long tenancy, avoided vacancy costs) as a specific, practical angle. Include a fallback plan (researching other units) in case negotiation fails, to show balanced advice.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 9, "task_number": 1, "task_key": "giving_advice", "task_title": "Giving Advice", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Your friend Owen has been asked by his manager to train a new employee, but the new hire seems to resent taking instructions from someone at the same level and keeps ignoring his advice, making mistakes that Owen then has to fix. Owen is frustrated and doesn't know whether to escalate it to their manager or handle it himself. He asks for your advice. What would you tell him?", "model_answer": "Owen, I'd try to handle this directly with the new hire before bringing in your manager, because escalating too early can make things feel more serious than they need to be and might damage the working relationship permanently. I'd suggest having a calm, private conversation where you're honest but not accusatory, something like explaining that you were asked to help them succeed and that repeated mistakes are creating extra work and stress for both of you. Ask directly if something is bothering them about the training process, since sometimes people resist instructions because they feel embarrassed or singled out, not because they actually disrespect you. Try adjusting your approach too, maybe explaining the reasoning behind instructions instead of just giving them, since some people respond better when they understand the why. Document specific mistakes and the guidance you gave beforehand, just in case the pattern continues, so you have concrete examples rather than a vague complaint. If after a genuine, direct conversation the behaviour doesn't change within a week or two, then it's completely reasonable to loop in your manager, since at that point it's affecting your own workload and performance too. Give the direct approach one real chance first, but don't feel guilty about escalating if it doesn't work.", "tips": "Recommend the less drastic option first (a direct conversation) before escalation, and explain why, to show reasoned judgment. Mention documentation as a practical, workplace-realistic detail that adds credibility.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 10, "task_number": 1, "task_key": "giving_advice", "task_title": "Giving Advice", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Your friend Camille has been training for months to run her first half marathon, but two weeks before race day she strained her ankle and her doctor advised her to rest instead of run. She's devastated and considers running anyway despite the risk of a worse injury. She asks for your advice. What would you tell her?", "model_answer": "Camille, I know how much this race meant to you after months of training, so I understand the temptation to push through, but I'd strongly advise against running on an injured ankle, honestly. A strain that gets worse could sideline you for months instead of just missing one event, and that would set back all the progress you've already made far more than skipping this race ever could. Instead, I'd focus on what you can control right now, which is actually recovering properly so you can come back stronger. Follow your doctor's advice closely, whether that's rest, physiotherapy, or specific exercises, and treat the recovery period as seriously as you treated your training. Many races also offer deferral options, letting you transfer your registration to a future date instead of losing it completely, so it's worth checking with the race organizers about that possibility. In the meantime, you could stay involved by volunteering at the race or cheering on the runners you trained with, which keeps you connected to the community without risking your ankle. Once you're fully healed, sign up for the next available half marathon with a realistic training plan, and you'll likely run it with even more determination than you would have this time.", "tips": "Directly discourage the risky action first, then pivot immediately to constructive alternatives like deferral options, so the answer doesn't feel like just a lecture. Mention emotional acknowledgment ('I know how much this meant') to sound genuinely supportive.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 11, "task_number": 2, "task_key": "personal_experience", "task_title": "Talking about a Personal Experience", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Describe a time when you had to learn a new skill quickly for a job or a school project. What was the skill, why did you need to learn it fast, and how did you go about learning it?", "model_answer": "A few years ago, I had to learn advanced spreadsheet formulas within just a couple of days, because my manager assigned me a budget report built entirely with complex functions I'd never used before, and it was due to senior leadership that same week. I started by watching a few short online tutorials focused on the exact functions in the existing file, rather than trying to learn everything from scratch. I also reached out to a colleague in finance, who walked me through the logic behind the calculations on a quick video call. Instead of memorizing every formula, I focused on understanding the pattern so I could adapt it to new data. By the second day, I updated the report confidently and even caught an error in the old version. That experience taught me learning under pressure is more about finding the right resources quickly than studying everything in depth.", "tips": "Follow a clear timeline: the trigger, the specific learning method, and the outcome. Keep to one concrete example rather than several vague ones, since 60 seconds only allows time for one well-developed story.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 12, "task_number": 2, "task_key": "personal_experience", "task_title": "Talking about a Personal Experience", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Describe a memorable meal you had with family or friends. Where was it, who was there, and why does it stand out in your memory?", "model_answer": "One meal that really stands out was a dinner my extended family put together at my grandmother's house the winter before she passed away. About fifteen of us crowded into her small kitchen and dining room, and everyone contributed a dish with some family history behind it, like my aunt's stew recipe passed down from her own mother. What made it memorable wasn't really the food, though it was delicious, but the stories that came out over the evening, things I'd never heard before about my grandmother's childhood and how she immigrated here decades ago. We stayed at the table for almost four hours, talking and laughing long after the plates were empty. Looking back, I'm grateful we gathered that particular night, since it turned out to be one of the last times the whole family was together with her.", "tips": "Emotional or reflective endings work well for this task type, since 'personal experience' prompts often reward genuine reflection, not just a factual description. Include sensory or specific details (the kitchen, the stew recipe) to sound authentic.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 13, "task_number": 2, "task_key": "personal_experience", "task_title": "Talking about a Personal Experience", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Describe a time you had a disagreement with a friend or family member. What was it about, and how did you eventually resolve it?", "model_answer": "A couple of years ago, my roommate and I had a serious disagreement about splitting household chores, because I felt I was doing most of the cleaning while she focused only on tasks she cared about, like the kitchen, while ignoring the bathroom completely. It built up for weeks until I finally brought it up directly one evening instead of quietly resenting her. At first she was defensive, saying she hadn't realized it was uneven, but once I gave specific examples, she agreed the split wasn't fair. We ended up creating a written chore schedule that rotated tasks weekly, so neither of us always got stuck with the jobs nobody enjoys. It wasn't a comfortable conversation to start, but avoiding it would only have made things worse. Since then, I try to address issues early rather than let frustration build up silently.", "tips": "Show the resolution process step by step (the confrontation, the reaction, the concrete solution) rather than jumping straight to 'we worked it out.' End with a lesson learned to demonstrate reflective language.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 14, "task_number": 2, "task_key": "personal_experience", "task_title": "Talking about a Personal Experience", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Describe a time when you helped someone in your community, either through volunteering or simply by lending a hand. What did you do, and how did it make you feel?", "model_answer": "Last winter, I noticed an elderly neighbour struggling to shovel her driveway after a heavy snowstorm, so I grabbed my shovel and cleared it for her without being asked. She was surprised and a little embarrassed at first, insisting she could manage, but once I explained I was already outside doing mine anyway, she gratefully accepted. It took about twenty extra minutes, but she later told me it would have taken her most of the afternoon. After that, I started checking her driveway automatically every time it snowed, and it became a small routine between us; she now leaves cookies on my porch occasionally as thanks. Honestly, it made me feel far more connected to my neighbourhood, and reminded me that small acts of help often matter more than we realize.", "tips": "Small, everyday acts of kindness work well here and are easier to describe vividly than large volunteering projects. Mention a follow-up detail (the ongoing routine, the cookies) to show the experience had a lasting impact.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 15, "task_number": 2, "task_key": "personal_experience", "task_title": "Talking about a Personal Experience", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Describe a time you moved to a new place, whether a new city, a new neighbourhood, or a new home. What was the experience like, and how did you adjust?", "model_answer": "About four years ago, I moved from a small town to a much larger city for a new job, and the adjustment was harder than I expected. Everything felt overwhelming at first, from navigating public transit to getting used to the noise outside my apartment at all hours. For the first month I felt pretty isolated, since I didn't know anyone and worked from home most days. What really helped was joining a local recreational volleyball league, which forced me to be around people regularly and eventually led to real friendships. I also made a point of exploring one new neighbourhood every weekend, which slowly made the city feel more familiar. Within about six months I genuinely felt at home, and now I actually prefer the city's energy to my hometown's quiet.", "tips": "Contrast the initial difficulty with the eventual adjustment to give the story a natural arc. Naming a specific strategy (joining a league, exploring neighbourhoods) gives concrete, gradable content rather than vague statements.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 16, "task_number": 2, "task_key": "personal_experience", "task_title": "Talking about a Personal Experience", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Describe a time when you had to work with a difficult person, either at school or at work. What made the situation challenging, and how did you handle it?", "model_answer": "During a college group project, I was paired with a classmate who consistently missed deadlines and rarely responded to messages, which put the rest of us under pressure as the due date approached. It was frustrating because our grade depended on everyone contributing equally, and his silence made it hard to plan around his part. Instead of complaining to the professor right away, I messaged him directly to ask if something was going on, figuring there might be a reason behind the pattern. It turned out he was dealing with a family emergency he hadn't mentioned. Once I understood that, our group redistributed his workload temporarily while checking in on him more gently. We finished the project successfully, and he was genuinely thankful. That taught me difficult behaviour often has an explanation you don't see at first.", "tips": "A twist ending (discovering a hidden reason for the behaviour) makes the story memorable and shows empathy, a strong quality to demonstrate in speaking responses. Keep the resolution realistic and specific rather than overly neat.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 17, "task_number": 2, "task_key": "personal_experience", "task_title": "Talking about a Personal Experience", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Describe a time you achieved a personal goal that took a long time or a lot of effort to reach. What was the goal, and what did the process teach you?", "model_answer": "One goal I worked toward for almost two years was becoming fluent enough in French to pass a certification exam for a promotion at work. Starting from a fairly basic level meant studying nearly every evening, taking weekend conversation classes, and forcing myself to watch French television without subtitles, even when I understood very little at first. There were moments I wanted to quit, especially after failing a practice exam about halfway through. Instead of giving up, I changed my approach, focusing more on speaking practice with a language partner than memorizing vocabulary lists. That shift made a huge difference, and I finally passed on my second attempt. Beyond the language skill itself, the process taught me that consistent small effort matters far more than occasional bursts of intense studying.", "tips": "Include a setback (failing the practice exam) before the eventual success, since this adds realism and gives you material for more complex sentence structures like contrast clauses. End with a broader lesson to tie the story together.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 18, "task_number": 2, "task_key": "personal_experience", "task_title": "Talking about a Personal Experience", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Describe a time when you received unexpected good news. What was the news, and how did you react?", "model_answer": "A couple of years ago, I received an email informing me I'd been selected for a scholarship I had applied for almost a year earlier and honestly forgotten about, assuming I hadn't been chosen. I remember reading the subject line while making breakfast and having to sit down, genuinely thinking it might be a mistake. Once I confirmed it was real, I called my parents immediately, and my mother actually started crying, since the scholarship covered a significant portion of my tuition for the following year. It completely changed my financial situation and let me cut back on the part-time hours I'd been working just to get by. What made it even more meaningful was that the application had asked about my long-term goals, so being chosen felt like real validation. I still keep that acceptance email saved.", "tips": "Physical or emotional reaction details (sitting down, calling parents) make the story vivid and natural sounding rather than a flat retelling of facts. A closing detail like keeping the email adds a nice personal touch.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 19, "task_number": 2, "task_key": "personal_experience", "task_title": "Talking about a Personal Experience", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Describe a trip or outing that didn't go as planned. What went wrong, and how did you deal with the situation?", "model_answer": "A few summers ago, my friends and I planned a weekend camping trip that went sideways almost immediately, since we arrived to discover our reservation had somehow been cancelled without notice. It was already late afternoon, and every nearby campground was fully booked for the long weekend, so we genuinely didn't know what to do. Instead of driving hours back home, we checked into a small motel in a town we'd never heard of before that day. It turned out to be one of the best decisions we made, because the town had a charming little lake, and we spent the next day kayaking and exploring local shops instead of hiking as planned. We still joke about it now as our accidental vacation, and honestly, it ended up more memorable than the trip we had planned.", "tips": "Stories where a problem leads to an unexpectedly positive outcome are engaging and easy to structure clearly. Use transition phrases like 'instead of' and 'it turned out' to link cause and effect smoothly.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 20, "task_number": 2, "task_key": "personal_experience", "task_title": "Talking about a Personal Experience", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Describe a time you gave a presentation, speech, or performance in front of an audience. How did you prepare, and how did it go?", "model_answer": "In my final year of college, I had to present my capstone research in front of a panel of professors and about fifty classmates, the largest audience I'd ever spoken to. I was genuinely nervous for weeks beforehand, so I prepared by rehearsing out loud at least ten times, timing myself to stay within the limit. I also practiced in front of my roommate and asked her to interrupt me with unexpected questions, to simulate what the panel might do. On the day, my hands shook slightly at the start, but focusing on the first memorized sentence helped me settle into a rhythm. Afterward, one of the professors told me it was one of the clearer presentations she'd seen that semester, which meant a lot given how anxious I'd been. That taught me thorough preparation is really the best cure for nervousness.", "tips": "Describe the preparation process in specific steps (rehearsing aloud, practicing with interruptions) since 'how did you prepare' is directly asked in the prompt. Mention the nervous feeling honestly, then how it changed, to add authenticity.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 21, "task_number": 3, "task_key": "describing_scene", "task_title": "Describing a Scene", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: A busy farmers market on a sunny Saturday morning in a city park. Several vendors under white canopy tents are selling fresh vegetables and fruit, a woman in an apron is offering a sample of cheese to a customer, a young child is pointing excitedly at a stall covered in colourful flowers, and a small line of people has formed at a coffee cart near the entrance. A man is loading bags of produce into a cloth tote, and a dog on a leash is sitting patiently beside its owner near one of the tables. Describe what is happening in this picture in as much detail as possible.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a lively farmers market outdoors in a city park on what looks like a bright, sunny Saturday morning. Several vendor tents are set up in a row, mostly selling fresh vegetables and fruit neatly arranged in wooden crates and baskets. In the foreground, a vendor wearing an apron appears to be handing a small sample of cheese to a customer, who looks like she's considering whether to buy some. Nearby, a young child is pointing excitedly toward a stall covered in colourful flowers, seeming fascinated by the display. Off to the side, a small line of people is waiting at a coffee cart near the entrance, probably grabbing a drink before browsing further. I can also see a man loading several bags of produce into a reusable cloth tote, suggesting he's nearly done shopping, and a dog sitting calmly on a leash beside its owner. Overall, it looks like a cheerful, community-focused scene.", "tips": "Move through the picture systematically (foreground to background, left to right, or by activity) rather than randomly, so your description sounds organized. Use present continuous tense ('is pointing', 'are selling') throughout since you're describing an ongoing scene.", "image": "speaking_images/task3_21_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: A busy farmers market on a sunny Saturday morning in a city park. Several vendors under white canopy tents are selling fresh vegetables and fruit, a woman in an apron is offering a sample of cheese to a customer, a young child is pointing excitedly at a stall covered in colourful flowers, and a small line of people has formed at a coffee cart near the entrance. A man is loading bags of produce into a cloth tote, and a dog on a leash is sitting patiently beside its owner near one of the tables."}, {"id": 22, "task_number": 3, "task_key": "describing_scene", "task_title": "Describing a Scene", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: A busy office break room during lunchtime. Three coworkers are seated at a round table, one is laughing at something on her phone, another is eating a sandwich while reading a document, and the third is stirring a cup of coffee while talking. Near the counter, a man is waiting for the microwave, tapping his foot impatiently, and a woman is filling a water bottle at the sink. A bulletin board on the wall is covered with notices and a birthday sign-up sheet. Describe what is happening in this picture in as much detail as possible.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a typical office break room during what looks like a busy lunch hour. At a round table, three coworkers appear to be spending their break together. One woman is laughing at something on her phone, clearly enjoying a funny video, while the coworker beside her eats a sandwich with one hand while reading a printed document with the other. The third seems to be mid-conversation, stirring a cup of coffee as she talks. Over near the counter, a man is standing in front of the microwave, tapping his foot as though a little impatient waiting for his food to heat up. Close to him, a woman is filling a reusable water bottle at the sink, seemingly just passing through. On the wall behind them, a bulletin board is covered with notices and what looks like a birthday sign-up sheet, suggesting a social office culture. Altogether, it feels like a relaxed but active midday scene.", "tips": "Break the room into zones (the table, the counter, the wall) to organize your description clearly within the time limit. Infer mood or personality from small details (tapping foot, laughing at phone) to add richer description rather than just listing objects.", "image": "speaking_images/task3_22_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: A busy office break room during lunchtime. Three coworkers are seated at a round table, one is laughing at something on her phone, another is eating a sandwich while reading a document, and the third is stirring a cup of coffee while talking. Near the counter, a man is waiting for the microwave, tapping his foot impatiently, and a woman is filling a water bottle at the sink. A bulletin board on the wall is covered with notices and a birthday sign-up sheet."}, {"id": 23, "task_number": 3, "task_key": "describing_scene", "task_title": "Describing a Scene", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: A public library reading area on a rainy afternoon. An elderly man is seated in an armchair near a large window, reading a newspaper with a cup of tea beside him. A teenager is sprawled on a beanbag chair with headphones on, scrolling through a tablet. A librarian is pushing a cart of books down an aisle, and two young students are whispering to each other at a study table covered in open notebooks. Rain is visible streaking down the window behind the armchair. Describe what is happening in this picture in as much detail as possible.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a quiet, cozy scene inside a public library on what appears to be a rainy afternoon, since rain is streaking down the large window behind one seating area. In an armchair near that window, an elderly man is comfortably seated, reading a newspaper with a cup of tea beside him, looking relaxed and unhurried. Not far from him, a teenager is sprawled casually on a beanbag chair, wearing headphones and scrolling through a tablet, seemingly more focused on entertainment than studying. Meanwhile, a librarian is pushing a cart of books down an aisle, likely returning items to their shelves. At a nearby study table covered in open notebooks, two young students appear to be whispering to each other, perhaps discussing an assignment. The overall mood feels calm and unhurried, with the grey weather outside making the warm library seem even more inviting.", "tips": "Weather or lighting details (the rain on the window) are useful for setting mood and should be mentioned early, since they frame the whole scene. Vary your verbs of position and action (seated, sprawled, pushing, whispering) instead of repeating 'is doing' constructions.", "image": "speaking_images/task3_23_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: A public library reading area on a rainy afternoon. An elderly man is seated in an armchair near a large window, reading a newspaper with a cup of tea beside him. A teenager is sprawled on a beanbag chair with headphones on, scrolling through a tablet. A librarian is pushing a cart of books down an aisle, and two young students are whispering to each other at a study table covered in open notebooks. Rain is visible streaking down the window behind the armchair."}, {"id": 24, "task_number": 3, "task_key": "describing_scene", "task_title": "Describing a Scene", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: A neighbourhood street during a weekend garage sale. A family has set up folding tables covered with old toys, books, and kitchen items in their driveway. A woman is negotiating the price of a lamp with a customer, while a young boy sits on the curb counting coins from a cash box. Across the street, two neighbours are chatting over a fence, and a cyclist is riding past slowly, glancing at the items for sale. A hand-painted sign reading garage sale is taped to a mailbox. Describe what is happening in this picture in as much detail as possible.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a typical weekend garage sale in a residential neighbourhood, with a family having set up folding tables in their driveway, covered with old toys, books, and kitchen items. In the middle of the scene, a woman appears to be negotiating the price of a lamp with a customer, gesturing as though explaining why it's worth the asking price. Nearby, a young boy sits on the curb, carefully counting coins from a cash box, likely tracking the sale's earnings. Across the street, two neighbours are chatting casually over a fence, seeming unbothered by the activity, as if it's a normal weekend occurrence. A cyclist is riding past slowly, glancing at the items, perhaps considering stopping to browse. A hand-painted sign taped to the mailbox reads garage sale, helping passersby know what's going on. Altogether, the scene captures a relaxed Saturday morning atmosphere.", "tips": "Note small narrative signals like a hand-painted sign, since mentioning them shows careful observation. Use words like 'appears to be' and 'seems' when interpreting intent, which is natural, hedged spoken English appropriate for describing a still image.", "image": "speaking_images/task3_24_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: A neighbourhood street during a weekend garage sale. A family has set up folding tables covered with old toys, books, and kitchen items in their driveway. A woman is negotiating the price of a lamp with a customer, while a young boy sits on the curb counting coins from a cash box. Across the street, two neighbours are chatting over a fence, and a cyclist is riding past slowly, glancing at the items for sale. A hand-painted sign reading garage sale is taped to a mailbox."}, {"id": 25, "task_number": 3, "task_key": "describing_scene", "task_title": "Describing a Scene", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: A crowded subway platform during the evening rush hour. Commuters in business attire are checking their phones and watches while waiting near the yellow safety line. A busker is playing a guitar near a pillar with an open case for tips in front of him. A mother is holding her toddler's hand tightly while glancing anxiously down the tunnel for the approaching train. An advertisement poster for a upcoming concert covers part of the wall behind them. Describe what is happening in this picture in as much detail as possible.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a crowded subway platform during what looks like the evening rush hour, with commuters in business attire standing near the yellow safety line, clearly waiting for the next train. Many are checking their phones or glancing at their watches, suggesting they're eager to get home or worried about being late. Near a support pillar, a busker is playing guitar with an open case in front of him, presumably hoping commuters will drop in a few coins. In the middle of the platform, a mother is holding her toddler's hand tightly while anxiously glancing down the tunnel, watching for the train and keeping her child away from the edge. Behind the crowd, part of the wall is covered by a poster for an upcoming concert. Overall, the scene feels energetic but slightly tense, capturing the familiar rush of a typical urban commute.", "tips": "Rush hour scenes lend themselves well to describing a general 'mood' at the end (tense, energetic, hurried), which adds a strong concluding sentence. Use safety-related vocabulary naturally (safety line, tunnel, platform edge) since it fits transit scenes.", "image": "speaking_images/task3_25_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: A crowded subway platform during the evening rush hour. Commuters in business attire are checking their phones and watches while waiting near the yellow safety line. A busker is playing a guitar near a pillar with an open case for tips in front of him. A mother is holding her toddler's hand tightly while glancing anxiously down the tunnel for the approaching train. An advertisement poster for a upcoming concert covers part of the wall behind them."}, {"id": 26, "task_number": 3, "task_key": "describing_scene", "task_title": "Describing a Scene", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: A community garden on a warm afternoon in early summer. An older woman kneels beside a raised garden bed, carefully transplanting seedlings into the soil with gloved hands. Two teenagers are filling a watering can from a large rain barrel nearby. A man in overalls is repairing a wooden trellis that has come loose, while a group of children run past chasing a butterfly near a row of sunflowers. A wheelbarrow full of mulch sits near the garden entrance. Describe what is happening in this picture in as much detail as possible.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a lively community garden scene on what looks like a warm afternoon in early summer, with several people tending different areas of the space. In the foreground, an older woman is kneeling beside a raised garden bed, wearing gloves as she carefully transplants seedlings into the soil. Not far from her, two teenagers appear to be filling a watering can from a rain barrel, likely preparing to water nearby plants. Meanwhile, a man in overalls is repairing a wooden trellis that has come loose, probably before climbing plants grow too heavy for it. In the background, a group of children run past excitedly, chasing a butterfly near a row of tall sunflowers. Near the entrance, a wheelbarrow full of mulch sits waiting to be spread. Overall, the picture captures a busy but peaceful community effort.", "tips": "Garden and outdoor scenes offer many small tasks happening simultaneously, so grouping people by their specific task (planting, watering, repairing, playing) keeps the description clear. Include a plausible reason for each action ('probably fixing it before...') to sound like natural inference rather than a flat list.", "image": "speaking_images/task3_26_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: A community garden on a warm afternoon in early summer. An older woman kneels beside a raised garden bed, carefully transplanting seedlings into the soil with gloved hands. Two teenagers are filling a watering can from a large rain barrel nearby. A man in overalls is repairing a wooden trellis that has come loose, while a group of children run past chasing a butterfly near a row of sunflowers. A wheelbarrow full of mulch sits near the garden entrance."}, {"id": 27, "task_number": 3, "task_key": "describing_scene", "task_title": "Describing a Scene", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: A busy airport departure lounge near a boarding gate. A businessman is typing quickly on his laptop while balancing it on his knees, and a family of four is spread across several seats, with the youngest child asleep on his mother's lap. An airline employee is speaking into a microphone at the gate counter, apparently making an announcement. Two friends are taking a selfie in front of a large window overlooking the runway, and a man is jogging with a rolling suitcase toward a different gate, looking at his watch. Describe what is happening in this picture in as much detail as possible.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a busy departure lounge at an airport, with a lot of activity near what looks like a boarding gate. On one side, a businessman is typing quickly on his laptop, balanced awkwardly on his knees, suggesting he's finishing work before his flight boards. Nearby, a family of four is spread across several seats, with the youngest child asleep on his mother's lap, probably worn out from waiting. At the gate counter, an airline employee is speaking into a microphone, seemingly making an announcement. Over by a window overlooking the runway, two friends are taking a selfie, looking excited about the trip. Meanwhile, in contrast to the relaxed mood elsewhere, a man is jogging with a rolling suitcase toward a different gate, glancing anxiously at his watch, which suggests he's worried about missing a connection. Altogether, the scene captures the mix of boredom and stress typical of a busy terminal.", "tips": "Airport scenes are great for showing contrast between calm and urgent moods within one picture, so highlight that contrast explicitly (as in 'in contrast to the relaxed mood elsewhere'). This kind of connecting phrase also demonstrates higher-level cohesive language.", "image": "speaking_images/task3_27_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: A busy airport departure lounge near a boarding gate. A businessman is typing quickly on his laptop while balancing it on his knees, and a family of four is spread across several seats, with the youngest child asleep on his mother's lap. An airline employee is speaking into a microphone at the gate counter, apparently making an announcement. Two friends are taking a selfie in front of a large window overlooking the runway, and a man is jogging with a rolling suitcase toward a different gate, looking at his watch."}, {"id": 28, "task_number": 3, "task_key": "describing_scene", "task_title": "Describing a Scene", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: A restaurant kitchen during the dinner rush. A head chef is shouting out an order while plating a dish with precise, quick movements. Two line cooks are working over sizzling pans at the stove, and a dishwasher is scrubbing a large stack of pots at a deep sink in the background. A server is standing at a pickup window holding a tray, waiting anxiously for the next order to be ready. Steam rises from several pots on the stove, and a ticket printer near the pass is spitting out a long strip of paper orders. Describe what is happening in this picture in as much detail as possible.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a restaurant kitchen in the middle of a chaotic dinner rush, with everyone clearly working under pressure. In the centre, the head chef appears to be shouting out an order while simultaneously plating a dish with quick, precise movements. Just behind him, two line cooks are working intensely over sizzling pans, with steam rising visibly from several pots, suggesting multiple dishes cooking at once. In the background, a dishwasher is scrubbing through a large stack of dirty pots at a deep sink, working steadily to keep up. Near a pickup window, a server is standing with an empty tray, waiting anxiously for the next order, eager to get food out before it cools. Close to the pass, a ticket printer is spitting out a long strip of paper orders, emphasizing how busy the kitchen is. Overall, the scene captures the intense teamwork required during peak hours.", "tips": "High-pressure workplace scenes benefit from vocabulary describing pace and intensity (chaotic, intense, fast-paced) used naturally throughout, not just at the end. Mentioning the ticket printer as visual evidence of workload is a good example of using a detail to support an inference.", "image": "speaking_images/task3_28_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: A restaurant kitchen during the dinner rush. A head chef is shouting out an order while plating a dish with precise, quick movements. Two line cooks are working over sizzling pans at the stove, and a dishwasher is scrubbing a large stack of pots at a deep sink in the background. A server is standing at a pickup window holding a tray, waiting anxiously for the next order to be ready. Steam rises from several pots on the stove, and a ticket printer near the pass is spitting out a long strip of paper orders."}, {"id": 29, "task_number": 3, "task_key": "describing_scene", "task_title": "Describing a Scene", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: A university lecture hall just before an exam begins. Rows of students sit spaced apart, most flipping through notes one last time, while one student has his head down on the desk, clearly exhausted. A teaching assistant walks down the aisle handing out exam booklets, and a clock on the wall shows the time is almost nine. Near the front, a professor is writing instructions on the whiteboard, and a backpack sits open on the floor beside an empty seat where someone has apparently stepped out. Describe what is happening in this picture in as much detail as possible.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a university lecture hall just moments before an exam, with a noticeably tense atmosphere. Rows of students are seated with some distance between them, most flipping through their notes one last time, likely reviewing key points before the papers are handed out. One student has his head down on the desk, looking exhausted, perhaps from staying up late studying. A teaching assistant is walking down an aisle distributing exam booklets, while a clock on the wall shows it's nearly nine, suggesting the exam is about to start. Near the front, the professor appears to be writing final instructions on the whiteboard. Off to one side, an open backpack sits beside an empty seat, suggesting that student has briefly stepped out. Altogether, the scene captures the nervous energy typical of exam day.", "tips": "Small clues like an open backpack beside an empty seat invite plausible storytelling ('suggests that student has stepped out'), which is exactly the kind of inference this task rewards. Reference the clock time explicitly to anchor the scene in a specific moment.", "image": "speaking_images/task3_29_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: A university lecture hall just before an exam begins. Rows of students sit spaced apart, most flipping through notes one last time, while one student has his head down on the desk, clearly exhausted. A teaching assistant walks down the aisle handing out exam booklets, and a clock on the wall shows the time is almost nine. Near the front, a professor is writing instructions on the whiteboard, and a backpack sits open on the floor beside an empty seat where someone has apparently stepped out."}, {"id": 30, "task_number": 3, "task_key": "describing_scene", "task_title": "Describing a Scene", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: A community hockey rink on a cold winter evening, lit by bright overhead lights. A group of young players in full gear are practicing passing drills at one end of the ice, while a coach blows a whistle and points toward the net. Parents are seated in the bleachers, some wrapped in blankets and holding cups of hot chocolate. A Zamboni is parked at one end of the rink, and a scoreboard clock shows a countdown for the next session. Describe what is happening in this picture in as much detail as possible.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a community hockey rink on what looks like a cold winter evening, brightly lit by overhead lights. At one end of the rink, a group of young players in full gear appear to be practicing passing drills, moving the puck back and forth with focused concentration. Nearby, a coach is blowing a whistle and pointing firmly toward the net, likely correcting the drill. Up in the bleachers, several parents are seated watching, some wrapped in blankets while holding cups of what looks like hot chocolate, bundled up against the cold arena. A Zamboni sits parked at one end, presumably waiting to resurface the ice once practice wraps up. A scoreboard clock displays a countdown, suggesting the session is nearing its end. Overall, the scene captures a typical, cozy winter evening at a community rink.", "tips": "Cold-weather or indoor-arena scenes give a chance to naturally use temperature and comfort vocabulary (bundled up, cozy) which adds descriptive richness. The countdown clock is a good detail to use for predicting what happens next in the scene's timeline.", "image": "speaking_images/task3_30_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: A community hockey rink on a cold winter evening, lit by bright overhead lights. A group of young players in full gear are practicing passing drills at one end of the ice, while a coach blows a whistle and points toward the net. Parents are seated in the bleachers, some wrapped in blankets and holding cups of hot chocolate. A Zamboni is parked at one end of the rink, and a scoreboard clock shows a countdown for the next session."}, {"id": 31, "task_number": 4, "task_key": "making_predictions", "task_title": "Making Predictions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: Two people stand beside a car parked on the shoulder of a highway, looking down at a flat front tire. One person is on the phone, gesturing with his free hand, while the other kneels beside the trunk, which is open, revealing a spare tire and a jack still wrapped in plastic. Dark storm clouds are gathering in the sky above them, and a few other cars are passing by in the distance. Describe what you think will happen next and explain why.", "model_answer": "I think the person on the phone is probably calling roadside assistance or a friend for help, since the spare tire and jack in the trunk still look brand new and unused, suggesting neither of them is confident about changing the tire themselves. Given the dark storm clouds overhead, I'd guess they want this sorted quickly before it rains, since changing a tire on a busy highway shoulder in bad weather would be uncomfortable and genuinely dangerous. I think whoever they're calling will either talk them through it or send someone out to help directly. If nobody responds quickly enough, they might attempt it themselves despite their hesitation, especially once the rain starts. Either way, I'd expect this to resolve within the next half hour, probably ending with them back on the road, just later than planned.", "tips": "Point to specific visual evidence (the plastic-wrapped jack) to justify your prediction rather than guessing randomly, since this task specifically rewards reasoning based on the image. Structure your answer as: prediction, evidence for it, and a brief alternative possibility.", "image": "speaking_images/task4_31_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: Two people stand beside a car parked on the shoulder of a highway, looking down at a flat front tire. One person is on the phone, gesturing with his free hand, while the other kneels beside the trunk, which is open, revealing a spare tire and a jack still wrapped in plastic. Dark storm clouds are gathering in the sky above them, and a few other cars are passing by in the distance."}, {"id": 32, "task_number": 4, "task_key": "making_predictions", "task_title": "Making Predictions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: A waiter is walking toward a table carrying a large tray loaded with several plates of food, while a young child at a nearby table suddenly stands up on his chair, reaching for a balloon tied to the back of it. The child's parents are looking down at their phones, apparently distracted. A server's cart loaded with dirty dishes sits partially blocking the aisle between the two tables. Describe what you think will happen next and explain why.", "model_answer": "I think there's a real chance of an accident happening in the next few seconds, mainly because the child is standing on an unstable chair reaching for the balloon, and his parents don't seem to notice since they're looking at their phones. Combined with the server's cart partially blocking the aisle, I'd guess the waiter with the heavy tray might need to swerve suddenly to avoid a collision, which could cause him to lose his balance and drop some plates. It's also possible the child could lose his footing, which would startle everyone and finally get his parents' attention. If nothing goes wrong, I'd expect the waiter to navigate around the cart successfully while the child gets pulled down by a parent just in time. Given how many things could go wrong at once, though, I think a minor mishap is actually the more likely outcome.", "tips": "This type of prompt often shows a scene with visible tension or danger, so it's fine to predict something slightly negative happening as long as you justify it with details from the picture. Mention a 'best case' alternative too, to show balanced reasoning rather than only one outcome.", "image": "speaking_images/task4_32_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: A waiter is walking toward a table carrying a large tray loaded with several plates of food, while a young child at a nearby table suddenly stands up on his chair, reaching for a balloon tied to the back of it. The child's parents are looking down at their phones, apparently distracted. A server's cart loaded with dirty dishes sits partially blocking the aisle between the two tables."}, {"id": 33, "task_number": 4, "task_key": "making_predictions", "task_title": "Making Predictions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: A man stands in front of an ATM machine, staring at the screen with a confused expression, holding his bank card in one hand and his phone in the other. Behind him, a small line of three people has formed, and one woman near the back is checking her watch impatiently. A printed receipt is sticking halfway out of the machine's slot. Describe what you think will happen next and explain why.", "model_answer": "I think the man at the ATM is probably confused because something unexpected happened with his transaction, maybe an error message appeared or the machine didn't dispense the cash he requested, which would explain his puzzled expression and why he's holding his phone, possibly ready to call his bank. Given that a receipt is already sticking out of the slot, the transaction did process in some way, so I'd guess he's trying to figure out whether the money actually came out. Since a small line has formed and one woman is checking her watch impatiently, I think the growing pressure will probably cause him to grab his card and step aside to sort it out elsewhere, or suggest the person behind him try a different machine. If he keeps taking too long, I'd expect the impatient woman to eventually say something or switch lines.", "tips": "The receipt detail is a subtle clue worth mentioning, since noticing small visual evidence and using it to build your prediction demonstrates careful observation. Predict how the other people in the scene (the line) will react too, not just the main subject.", "image": "speaking_images/task4_33_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: A man stands in front of an ATM machine, staring at the screen with a confused expression, holding his bank card in one hand and his phone in the other. Behind him, a small line of three people has formed, and one woman near the back is checking her watch impatiently. A printed receipt is sticking halfway out of the machine's slot."}, {"id": 34, "task_number": 4, "task_key": "making_predictions", "task_title": "Making Predictions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: A woman is standing at a bus stop checking her phone with a worried expression, while the digital sign above the stop shows the next bus is delayed by twenty-five minutes. Dark clouds are rolling in overhead, and she is holding a large folder of documents under one arm along with an umbrella that isn't opened. A taxi with its available light on is approaching slowly in the distance. Describe what you think will happen next and explain why.", "model_answer": "I think the woman is likely worried about being late for something important, given that she's holding a folder of documents, suggesting she might be heading to a job interview or an appointment she can't afford to miss. With the bus delayed by twenty-five minutes and dark clouds gathering, she's weighing her options quickly, and the taxi approaching in the distance makes me think she'll probably flag it down instead of waiting longer. Taking a taxi would let her avoid both the delay and getting caught in the rain, the more practical choice given how stressed she looks. If she doesn't catch the taxi's attention in time, I'd expect her to open her umbrella once it starts raining and keep waiting anxiously. Overall, I think time pressure and the weather together will push her toward the taxi.", "tips": "Combine two pieces of evidence (the delay sign and the approaching taxi) into one coherent prediction, since strong predictions usually connect multiple details rather than focusing on just one. State a clear main prediction first, then a fallback scenario.", "image": "speaking_images/task4_34_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: A woman is standing at a bus stop checking her phone with a worried expression, while the digital sign above the stop shows the next bus is delayed by twenty-five minutes. Dark clouds are rolling in overhead, and she is holding a large folder of documents under one arm along with an umbrella that isn't opened. A taxi with its available light on is approaching slowly in the distance."}, {"id": 35, "task_number": 4, "task_key": "making_predictions", "task_title": "Making Predictions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: In a classroom, a teacher is handing back graded tests to students, and one student is staring down at his paper with a shocked expression, covering his mouth with his hand. Nearby, his friend is leaning over trying to see the grade, grinning. The classroom clock shows there are only a few minutes left before the bell rings, and several other students are already packing their backpacks. Describe what you think will happen next and explain why.", "model_answer": "I think the student staring at his test with a shocked expression probably received either a much better or worse grade than expected, and based on his hand covering his mouth, it seems more likely he's genuinely surprised rather than disappointed, suggesting it could actually be good news. His friend leaning over, still grinning, will probably find out within the next few seconds, and I'd guess he'll react with excitement or sympathy depending on the grade. Since the clock shows only a few minutes before the bell, and other students are already packing up, the teacher will likely finish handing back tests quickly, with little time for discussion. Once the bell rings, I'd expect the shocked student to show his friend the test up close, and the two will probably talk about it while walking out together.", "tips": "A slightly ambiguous expression (shocked could mean good or bad news) gives you room to reason through both possibilities before settling on the more likely one, which shows strong critical thinking. Use the classroom clock detail to predict the immediate next few minutes realistically.", "image": "speaking_images/task4_35_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: In a classroom, a teacher is handing back graded tests to students, and one student is staring down at his paper with a shocked expression, covering his mouth with his hand. Nearby, his friend is leaning over trying to see the grade, grinning. The classroom clock shows there are only a few minutes left before the bell rings, and several other students are already packing their backpacks."}, {"id": 36, "task_number": 4, "task_key": "making_predictions", "task_title": "Making Predictions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: A hiker stands at a fork in a wilderness trail, looking down at a paper map in one hand and a phone in the other, with a confused expression. One path is marked with a faded, hard-to-read sign, while the other has no sign at all. The sky is beginning to darken with the approach of evening, and the hiker's backpack looks heavy, with a water bottle attached to the side that appears nearly empty. Describe what you think will happen next and explain why.", "model_answer": "I think the hiker is facing a genuinely difficult decision, since neither the faded sign nor the unmarked path gives clear direction, and checking both a map and his phone suggests he's not fully confident in either, possibly because of weak signal in the wilderness. Given the darkening sky and his nearly empty water bottle, time pressure will probably force a decision fairly quickly rather than continued deliberation. I'd guess he'll ultimately trust the paper map over the faded sign, since a map is more reliable if his phone loses signal. If he chooses wrong, I think he'll realize the mistake within a few minutes once the trail doesn't match the map, and he'd probably backtrack quickly given the approaching darkness and low water. Overall, I expect caution to guide his decision more than convenience.", "tips": "Outdoor or wilderness scenes with implied risk are good opportunities to explain WHY a wrong decision would be dangerous, which deepens your reasoning beyond a simple guess. Reference resource details (the near-empty water bottle) to justify a sense of urgency in your prediction.", "image": "speaking_images/task4_36_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: A hiker stands at a fork in a wilderness trail, looking down at a paper map in one hand and a phone in the other, with a confused expression. One path is marked with a faded, hard-to-read sign, while the other has no sign at all. The sky is beginning to darken with the approach of evening, and the hiker's backpack looks heavy, with a water bottle attached to the side that appears nearly empty."}, {"id": 37, "task_number": 4, "task_key": "making_predictions", "task_title": "Making Predictions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: At a busy checkout counter in a grocery store, a customer is patting down his pockets with a worried look, having just realized he can't find his wallet, while his cart full of groceries sits on the belt already partially scanned. The cashier is waiting with her hand paused over the register, and a line of three customers behind him is starting to look impatient, with one person glancing at the clock on the wall. Describe what you think will happen next and explain why.", "model_answer": "The customer has clearly just realized he can't find his wallet, given how he's patting down his pockets, and since his groceries are already partially scanned, this creates an awkward situation for him and the cashier. I think he'll probably ask her to hold his order while he checks his car or searches his bag more thoroughly. Given that the line behind him is growing impatient, with one person glancing at the clock, I'd guess the cashier will offer to set his items aside briefly, or suggest he try a mobile payment app instead. If he genuinely can't find any way to pay, he'll likely leave some items behind and pay for what he can afford, or ask her to void the transaction and come back later. Either way, I expect this to resolve within a couple of minutes.", "tips": "Suggest a practical, real-world solution the cashier might offer (like setting the order aside) rather than only focusing on the customer's problem, since this shows awareness of how the whole scene would likely unfold. Keep the pacing realistic ('within the next couple of minutes') to sound like a grounded prediction.", "image": "speaking_images/task4_37_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: At a busy checkout counter in a grocery store, a customer is patting down his pockets with a worried look, having just realized he can't find his wallet, while his cart full of groceries sits on the belt already partially scanned. The cashier is waiting with her hand paused over the register, and a line of three customers behind him is starting to look impatient, with one person glancing at the clock on the wall."}, {"id": 38, "task_number": 4, "task_key": "making_predictions", "task_title": "Making Predictions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: In an office, two coworkers stand near a printer that has jammed, with a red error light blinking and a stack of urgent-looking documents waiting nearby. One coworker is pulling gently at a sheet of paper stuck inside the machine, while the other looks anxiously at her watch and then toward a conference room where several people are visible sitting around a table, apparently waiting. Describe what you think will happen next and explain why.", "model_answer": "I think these documents are needed urgently for the meeting in the conference room, since one coworker keeps glancing at her watch and then toward the people already waiting inside, suggesting real time pressure. Given that the printer's error light is blinking and someone is already gently pulling at the stuck paper, I'd guess they're trying to fix the jam quickly rather than restarting the job, since that would waste more time. If the jam clears within a minute or two, they'll rush the documents in a little late but without major consequences. If the paper tears instead, I'd expect one of them to run to a different printer, or ask someone in the meeting to pull up the documents digitally. Either way, given how anxious she looks, I think they'll prioritize speed over a perfect fix.", "tips": "Offer two branching outcomes (the jam clears quickly vs. it doesn't) since printer or technical-failure scenes often have a clear fork in what happens next, and covering both fills the response time naturally.", "image": "speaking_images/task4_38_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: In an office, two coworkers stand near a printer that has jammed, with a red error light blinking and a stack of urgent-looking documents waiting nearby. One coworker is pulling gently at a sheet of paper stuck inside the machine, while the other looks anxiously at her watch and then toward a conference room where several people are visible sitting around a table, apparently waiting."}, {"id": 39, "task_number": 4, "task_key": "making_predictions", "task_title": "Making Predictions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: A couple stands in front of a real estate agent inside an empty apartment, looking at each other with uncertain expressions after the agent has just mentioned something while pointing to a paper in her hand. Moisture staining is visible on the ceiling in the corner of the room behind them, though it seems the agent hasn't pointed it out. A moving truck is parked outside, visible through the window, with a mover unloading a box labeled fragile. Describe what you think will happen next and explain why.", "model_answer": "I think the couple's uncertain expressions suggest the agent just mentioned something concerning, possibly about price, lease terms, or a condition of the apartment, and since there's visible moisture staining on the ceiling that the agent hasn't pointed out, I have a feeling that issue hasn't been disclosed yet. If the couple notices the staining themselves in the next few minutes, they'll likely ask the agent about it directly, since water damage is a serious concern. Given that a moving truck is already outside with a mover unloading boxes, they've likely committed to this apartment already, which makes discovering a problem now particularly stressful. I think there's a good chance they'll pause to ask pointed questions before letting the movers bring in more belongings. If the agent can't give a satisfying explanation, I'd expect them to reconsider the whole deal.", "tips": "Notice details the people IN the picture might not have noticed yet (the ceiling stain), since predicting how they'll react once they DO notice is a strong, specific type of prediction. Tie the moving truck detail into the stakes of the situation to explain why this moment matters.", "image": "speaking_images/task4_39_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: A couple stands in front of a real estate agent inside an empty apartment, looking at each other with uncertain expressions after the agent has just mentioned something while pointing to a paper in her hand. Moisture staining is visible on the ceiling in the corner of the room behind them, though it seems the agent hasn't pointed it out. A moving truck is parked outside, visible through the window, with a mover unloading a box labeled fragile."}, {"id": 40, "task_number": 4, "task_key": "making_predictions", "task_title": "Making Predictions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: At a soccer field, a player is running toward the goal with the ball, closely followed by a defender reaching out to intervene, while the goalkeeper crouches low, watching intently and shifting his weight to one side. The scoreboard shows the score is tied with only two minutes left on the clock, and spectators in the stands are on their feet, some with hands over their mouths. Describe what you think will happen next and explain why.", "model_answer": "Given how close the defender is to the player with the ball, I think there's a strong chance he'll manage to at least disrupt the shot, either deflecting the ball or forcing the attacker to rush and lose accuracy. The goalkeeper is already crouched low and shifting his weight to one side, suggesting he's anticipating the shot's direction, so he has a reasonable chance of making the save. Considering the score is tied with only two minutes left, both teams are under enormous pressure, which could make the attacker either more determined or more likely to rush and make a mistake. Based on the spectators already on their feet with hands over their mouths, everyone senses this could be a decisive moment. I'd guess the likely outcome is a close, dramatic play, either a late goal or a crucial save keeping the game tied.", "tips": "Sports scenes work well for predictions because you can discuss two competing outcomes (goal vs. save) with roughly equal plausibility, which naturally fills the response time. Reference the scoreboard and time remaining explicitly, since it raises the stakes and justifies why the moment is tense.", "image": "speaking_images/task4_40_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: At a soccer field, a player is running toward the goal with the ball, closely followed by a defender reaching out to intervene, while the goalkeeper crouches low, watching intently and shifting his weight to one side. The scoreboard shows the score is tied with only two minutes left on the clock, and spectators in the stands are on their feet, some with hands over their mouths."}, {"id": 41, "task_number": 5, "task_key": "comparing_persuading", "task_title": "Comparing and Persuading", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Your book club is choosing a venue for its monthly meeting. Picture A: a small independent bookstore with a cozy reading nook, mismatched armchairs, soft lamps, and a shelf of tea and coffee members can help themselves to. Picture B: a bright meeting room at the public library with long tables, sturdy chairs, a whiteboard, and large windows overlooking the parking lot. You need to convince the club organizer, Priya, which venue the group should book for next month. Choose one picture and persuade Priya why it is the better choice.", "model_answer": "Priya, I think we should book the bookstore for next month's meeting instead of the library room. The atmosphere there just fits what a book club is supposed to feel like. Those armchairs and the soft lighting make people want to settle in and actually talk about the novel, rather than sitting stiffly at a long table like we're in a business meeting. It's also a two-way benefit, since the store gets more foot traffic when we're there, and a few members have already mentioned they'd love to browse afterward and maybe pick up next month's pick on the spot. The tea and coffee are a nice touch too, since nobody has to worry about bringing their own snacks. The library room is practical, sure, but it feels a little cold and impersonal for a group that meets to bond over stories. I really think the bookstore will make people look forward to coming every month.", "tips": "Name the person you are persuading right away and use their name again near the end to keep the response conversational. Give at least two distinct reasons rather than repeating one idea in different words, and briefly acknowledge the other option before dismissing it.", "image": "speaking_images/task5_41_a.jpg", "image_2": "speaking_images/task5_41_b.jpg", "image_description": "a small independent bookstore with a cozy reading nook, mismatched armchairs, soft lamps, and a shelf of tea and coffee members can help themselves to.", "image_2_description": "a bright meeting room at the public library with long tables, sturdy chairs, a whiteboard, and large windows overlooking the parking lot."}, {"id": 42, "task_number": 5, "task_key": "comparing_persuading", "task_title": "Comparing and Persuading", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Your family is picking a pet for the household. Picture A: a calm, medium-sized dog lying on a rug, already leash-trained, that needs two walks a day. Picture B: a pair of sociable budgies in a large cage by a sunny window, that need their cage cleaned weekly but no walks. You need to convince your partner, Marc, which pet the family should get. Choose one and persuade Marc why it is the better choice.", "model_answer": "Marc, I really think we should go with the budgies over the dog. Between your work schedule and the kids' after-school activities, I honestly don't know when we'd fit in two walks a day, every single day, rain or shine. The budgies only need their cage cleaned once a week, which we can easily split between us on a weekend morning. They're also social birds, so having a pair means they'll keep each other entertained even when we're busy, and the kids would still get to interact with them and learn some responsibility without the pressure of a dog needing constant attention. I know you've always pictured a dog running around the yard, but realistically our lifestyle right now just isn't set up for that. The budgies give us companionship and colour in the house without the guilt of leaving an animal alone or rushing home for a walk.", "tips": "Bring in a specific, relatable detail from daily life (schedules, kids, workload) to make the persuasion feel grounded rather than generic. Address the listener's likely counterargument directly, as this response does with the dog preference, to sound more convincing.", "image": "speaking_images/task5_42_a.jpg", "image_2": "speaking_images/task5_42_b.jpg", "image_description": "a calm, medium-sized dog lying on a rug, already leash-trained, that needs two walks a day.", "image_2_description": "a pair of sociable budgies in a large cage by a sunny window, that need their cage cleaned weekly but no walks."}, {"id": 43, "task_number": 5, "task_key": "comparing_persuading", "task_title": "Comparing and Persuading", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Your office is choosing a spot for the annual team lunch. Picture A: a busy downtown steakhouse with white tablecloths, a set three-course menu, and a fairly high price per person. Picture B: a casual food hall with a dozen different stalls, communal seating, and a wide range of cuisines at lower prices. You need to convince your manager, Alan, which venue to book. Choose one and persuade Alan why it is the better choice.", "model_answer": "Alan, for this year's team lunch I'd really push for the food hall instead of the steakhouse. The biggest reason is that our team is quite diverse in what people like to eat, and a few colleagues have dietary restrictions that a fixed three-course menu just doesn't handle well. At the food hall, everyone can pick exactly what they want, whether that's ramen, tacos, or a salad bowl, so nobody ends up picking at a plate they didn't really want. It's also considerably cheaper per person, which means we could put the leftover budget toward a fun activity afterward, like mini golf or an escape room. The communal seating actually encourages people to mix and chat with teammates they don't usually sit near, which fits the whole point of a team lunch better than a formal table where people just talk to whoever's beside them. I think the team would genuinely have more fun there.", "tips": "Tie your reasoning to the underlying purpose of the event (team bonding, budget, inclusivity), not just surface preferences like taste. Mention a concrete secondary benefit, such as leftover budget or better mixing among colleagues, to strengthen the persuasion.", "image": "speaking_images/task5_43_a.jpg", "image_2": "speaking_images/task5_43_b.jpg", "image_description": "a busy downtown steakhouse with white tablecloths, a set three-course menu, and a fairly high price per person.", "image_2_description": "a casual food hall with a dozen different stalls, communal seating, and a wide range of cuisines at lower prices."}, {"id": 44, "task_number": 5, "task_key": "comparing_persuading", "task_title": "Comparing and Persuading", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Your neighbourhood association is choosing new equipment for the community park. Picture A: a modern splash pad with water jets and a rubberized surface, aimed mainly at young children. Picture B: an outdoor fitness circuit with pull-up bars, resistance stations, and a stretching area, aimed at teens and adults. You need to convince the association's chairperson, Denise, which option the park should install. Choose one and persuade Denise why it is the better choice.", "model_answer": "Denise, I'd like to make the case for the outdoor fitness circuit rather than the splash pad. Looking at our neighbourhood, we actually have more teenagers and working adults than families with very young kids, and right now there's nowhere nearby for them to exercise outside without driving to a gym. A fitness circuit would give that whole group something genuinely useful, and it's usable in almost every season, whereas a splash pad only really gets used for a few months in summer and then just sits idle the rest of the year. Maintenance is also a factor, since splash pads need regular water and plumbing upkeep, while fitness equipment mainly just needs occasional inspection. I know a splash pad sounds like more fun on paper, but in terms of who would actually benefit day to day and how the investment holds up over the whole year, the fitness circuit is the smarter choice for our community.", "tips": "Use demographic or usage-pattern reasoning (who actually lives in the area, seasonal usability) to make the argument feel researched rather than purely personal preference. Bring up a practical downside of the option you're rejecting, like seasonal use or maintenance cost.", "image": "speaking_images/task5_44_a.jpg", "image_2": "speaking_images/task5_44_b.jpg", "image_description": "a modern splash pad with water jets and a rubberized surface, aimed mainly at young children.", "image_2_description": "an outdoor fitness circuit with pull-up bars, resistance stations, and a stretching area, aimed at teens and adults."}, {"id": 45, "task_number": 5, "task_key": "comparing_persuading", "task_title": "Comparing and Persuading", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "You and a friend are planning a weekend getaway. Picture A: a rustic cabin by a lake, with canoeing, a campfire pit, and no cell signal. Picture B: a boutique hotel in a small historic town, with walkable shops, cafes, and a museum nearby. You need to convince your friend, Sam, which trip to book. Choose one and persuade Sam why it is the better choice.", "model_answer": "Sam, I really think we should book the historic town hotel instead of the cabin this time. Don't get me wrong, the lake looks beautiful, but we both agreed a while back that we wanted this trip to actually feel restful, and I think being completely cut off with no cell signal would stress you out more than relax you, especially with your parents' health situation right now. The town gives us the best of both worlds, since we can still unplug and wander around at our own pace, but we're not stranded if something comes up. Plus, honestly, neither of us has ever canoed before, and I'd rather not spend half the trip figuring that out. The museum and the little cafes mean we'll actually have things to do even if the weather turns bad, which the cabin doesn't really offer. I think we'll come back from the town trip more relaxed and with better stories.", "tips": "Reference something specific about the listener's situation or past conversation, as this answer does with the parents' health, to make the persuasion feel personal and well-reasoned rather than generic. Include a practical backup argument, like a weather contingency, to round out the case.", "image": "speaking_images/task5_45_a.jpg", "image_2": "speaking_images/task5_45_b.jpg", "image_description": "a rustic cabin by a lake, with canoeing, a campfire pit, and no cell signal.", "image_2_description": "a boutique hotel in a small historic town, with walkable shops, cafes, and a museum nearby."}, {"id": 46, "task_number": 5, "task_key": "comparing_persuading", "task_title": "Comparing and Persuading", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Your company needs a new laptop model for its employees. Picture A: a lightweight ultrabook with a long battery life but limited ports and moderate processing power. Picture B: a bulkier workstation laptop with excellent processing power and many ports, but a shorter battery life and heavier weight. You need to convince your IT director, Farah, which model to purchase. Choose one and persuade Farah why it is the better choice.", "model_answer": "Farah, for this purchase I'd recommend the lightweight ultrabook over the workstation model. Most of our staff spend their day in meetings, moving between floors, or working from client sites, so battery life and portability matter far more to them than raw processing power they'll rarely max out. Carrying a heavy workstation laptop around all day, plus a charger because the battery won't last, is exactly the kind of small friction that adds up and frustrates employees. The limited ports aren't really a dealbreaker either, since a simple USB-C dock at each desk solves that completely and costs almost nothing compared to the laptop itself. Unless a department specifically does heavy video editing or data modelling, which we could handle as a separate exception, the ultrabook covers what ninety percent of our team actually needs. I think it'll improve day-to-day comfort without sacrificing anything most people would notice.", "tips": "Ground your argument in the actual needs of the users (how staff work day to day) rather than just comparing specs abstractly. Offer a solution to the weaker point of your chosen option, such as suggesting a dock for limited ports, to preempt objections.", "image": "speaking_images/task5_46_a.jpg", "image_2": "speaking_images/task5_46_b.jpg", "image_description": "a lightweight ultrabook with a long battery life but limited ports and moderate processing power.", "image_2_description": "a bulkier workstation laptop with excellent processing power and many ports, but a shorter battery life and heavier weight."}, {"id": 47, "task_number": 5, "task_key": "comparing_persuading", "task_title": "Comparing and Persuading", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Your school is choosing a guest speaker for graduation. Picture A: a well-known local entrepreneur who built a small chain of businesses from scratch and speaks about resilience. Picture B: a retired astronaut with an impressive career who now gives polished, widely praised motivational talks. You need to convince the graduation committee lead, Mr. Okafor, which speaker to invite. Choose one and persuade him why it is the better choice.", "model_answer": "Mr. Okafor, I'd like to push for the local entrepreneur rather than the astronaut for this year's graduation. The astronaut's story is impressive, no question, but it can feel a little distant to our students, since most of them will never do anything remotely like going to space. The entrepreneur's journey is much closer to what our graduates are actually facing, things like starting with almost nothing, dealing with failure, and slowly building something in this very city. That kind of story tends to stick with students longer because they can actually picture themselves in it. There's also a practical angle, since inviting someone local keeps costs and logistics much simpler than flying in and accommodating a nationally known figure. I think our students will walk away feeling genuinely motivated rather than just entertained for an hour, and that's really the point of a graduation speech.", "tips": "Contrast relatability against prestige explicitly, since this is the classic tension in this kind of comparison, and explain why relatability wins for this specific audience. A brief nod to logistics or cost can make the argument feel more complete without dominating the response.", "image": "speaking_images/task5_47_a.jpg", "image_2": "speaking_images/task5_47_b.jpg", "image_description": "a well-known local entrepreneur who built a small chain of businesses from scratch and speaks about resilience.", "image_2_description": "a retired astronaut with an impressive career who now gives polished, widely praised motivational talks."}, {"id": 48, "task_number": 5, "task_key": "comparing_persuading", "task_title": "Comparing and Persuading", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "You and your roommate are choosing a new couch for the living room. Picture A: a sleek grey leather couch, stylish but firm and cool to sit on, with a higher price tag. Picture B: a large, soft fabric sectional in a warm colour, very comfortable but harder to keep clean. You need to convince your roommate, Jess, which couch to buy. Choose one and persuade her why it is the better choice.", "model_answer": "Jess, I really think we should go with the fabric sectional instead of the leather couch. We both work long hours, and when we finally get home, what we actually want is somewhere to sink into and relax, not something firm and a bit cold that looks nice but isn't that comfortable to lounge on. The sectional gives us way more seating too, which matters since we're always having friends over for movie nights. I know the cleaning concern is real, but a few washable throw covers and a fabric protector spray would handle most of that, and honestly it's a much smaller hassle than the price difference on the leather option. We'd be saving a decent amount of money that could go toward the coffee table we've been wanting. At the end of the day, comfort and how much we'll actually use it should matter more than how sleek it looks in photos.", "tips": "Weigh comfort against practicality directly and explain which one matters more given how the space is actually used, such as frequent guests or long workdays. Offer a simple fix for the downside of your choice, like washable covers, to make the recommendation feel realistic.", "image": "speaking_images/task5_48_a.jpg", "image_2": "speaking_images/task5_48_b.jpg", "image_description": "a sleek grey leather couch, stylish but firm and cool to sit on, with a higher price tag.", "image_2_description": "a large, soft fabric sectional in a warm colour, very comfortable but harder to keep clean."}, {"id": 49, "task_number": 5, "task_key": "comparing_persuading", "task_title": "Comparing and Persuading", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Your city council is deciding how to use a vacant downtown lot. Picture A: a community garden with raised planting beds, a small greenhouse, and space for workshops. Picture B: a paid parking lot generating revenue for the city, with space for about forty cars. You need to convince the council member, Councillor Reyes, which use to approve. Choose one and persuade her why it is the better choice.", "model_answer": "Councillor Reyes, I'd urge the council to approve the community garden rather than another parking lot for that space. Downtown already has several paid lots within a couple of blocks, so forty more spaces would barely make a dent in parking demand, but we have almost no green space in that part of the city for residents to actually gather in. A garden brings people together in a way pavement never will, giving families a place to grow their own food, hosting workshops for seniors and kids alike, and honestly making that whole block feel more welcoming instead of just another stretch of asphalt. It also fits with the sustainability goals the council has been talking about for the last two budget cycles, since it reduces heat buildup and supports local food production. Yes, it won't generate direct revenue like a parking lot would, but the community benefit and the improved quality of life for residents are worth far more in the long run.", "tips": "Compare the two options against a bigger-picture goal, such as existing city priorities or resource scarcity, rather than only listing individual benefits. Acknowledge the financial trade-off honestly, as this answer does with lost parking revenue, then explain why the other benefit outweighs it.", "image": "speaking_images/task5_49_a.jpg", "image_2": "speaking_images/task5_49_b.jpg", "image_description": "a community garden with raised planting beds, a small greenhouse, and space for workshops.", "image_2_description": "a paid parking lot generating revenue for the city, with space for about forty cars."}, {"id": 50, "task_number": 5, "task_key": "comparing_persuading", "task_title": "Comparing and Persuading", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Your family is deciding on a summer activity for the kids. Picture A: a two-week structured day camp with scheduled sports, crafts, and field trips, run by trained counsellors. Picture B: an unstructured summer at home with a flexible schedule, occasional playdates, and free time to pursue their own interests. You need to convince your spouse, Lena, which option is better for the kids this year. Choose one and persuade her why it is the better choice.", "model_answer": "Lena, I think we should sign the kids up for the day camp this summer instead of keeping things unstructured at home. Last summer they were bored by the second week, and honestly, both of us ended up refereeing arguments between them far more than either of us expected. The camp gives them a set routine, real physical activity every day, and a chance to make friends outside their usual school group, which they've been a bit short on since switching schools. Trained counsellors also means they're getting proper supervision and guidance we simply can't provide while we're both working from home trying to focus on our own jobs. I know free, unstructured time has its own value, and we can still build some of that into evenings and weekends, but for the bulk of the summer I think the structure and social exposure the camp offers will genuinely benefit them more this particular year.", "tips": "Reference a concrete past experience, like last summer's boredom, to justify why this year should be handled differently. Show balance by acknowledging the value of the option you're not choosing before explaining why circumstances favour the other one.", "image": "speaking_images/task5_50_a.jpg", "image_2": "speaking_images/task5_50_b.jpg", "image_description": "a two-week structured day camp with scheduled sports, crafts, and field trips, run by trained counsellors.", "image_2_description": "an unstructured summer at home with a flexible schedule, occasional playdates, and free time to pursue their own interests."}, {"id": 51, "task_number": 6, "task_key": "difficult_situation", "task_title": "Dealing with a Difficult Situation", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "You borrowed your coworker's expensive noise-cancelling headphones for a work trip and accidentally cracked one of the earcups. You need to tell your coworker what happened and how you plan to make it right. Leave a voicemail explaining the situation.", "model_answer": "Hi, it's me. I wanted to call before you noticed on your own, because I have to be honest with you about something. While I was on the work trip, I dropped your headphones getting off the shuttle bus, and one of the earcups actually cracked. I feel really bad about it since I know how much you liked them and how careful you always are with your own things. I've already looked into it, and I found the exact replacement part online, so I'd like to either order it and fix them myself or just cover the full repair cost, whichever you'd prefer. If neither option works for you, I'm also happy to just replace them outright with a brand new pair, since I know how much you rely on them for work. Please call me back whenever you get a chance so we can sort out what makes the most sense for you. Again, I'm really sorry about this.", "tips": "Open by admitting the problem directly and clearly instead of burying it in small talk, since a difficult message loses credibility if it delays the bad news. Offer more than one concrete solution so the listener feels you've already thought it through rather than just apologizing.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 52, "task_number": 6, "task_key": "difficult_situation", "task_title": "Dealing with a Difficult Situation", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Your landlord has scheduled a plumbing inspection for the exact same afternoon as your best friend's wedding, which you cannot miss. You need to call your landlord to ask for the appointment to be rescheduled. Leave a voicemail explaining the situation.", "model_answer": "Hi, this is calling about the plumbing inspection you scheduled for this Saturday afternoon. I just realized when I checked my calendar that it's the exact same time as my best friend's wedding, which I'm actually part of the wedding party for, so unfortunately I really can't be there to let the plumber in. I completely understand these appointments can be hard to shift around, so I want to make this as easy as possible on your end. I'm available any morning next week before ten, or any afternoon after four, and I could also leave a spare key with my neighbour in unit 4B if that would work better for your schedule instead of me being there in person. I'm sorry for the short notice on this, since I only just noticed the conflict myself. Please give me a call back or send a text with whatever time works, and I'll make sure I'm available or arrange the key.", "tips": "Explain the reason for the conflict briefly but don't over-apologize or ramble, since a concise, respectful explanation sounds more confident. Offer a specific alternative, like a key with a neighbour, to show you're trying to minimize the inconvenience to the other person.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 53, "task_number": 6, "task_key": "difficult_situation", "task_title": "Dealing with a Difficult Situation", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "You realize that a project you submitted to your manager last week contains a data error that could affect a client presentation happening tomorrow morning. You need to inform your manager right away. Leave a voicemail explaining the situation.", "model_answer": "Hi, it's me, and I'm sorry to call outside of office hours, but I wanted to flag something before tomorrow's client presentation. While I was double-checking the figures tonight, I noticed that the quarterly growth numbers in the report I submitted last week were pulled from the wrong spreadsheet tab, so the percentages are actually off by a fair amount. I've already gone back and recalculated the correct figures using the right source data, and I can have a corrected version ready and in your inbox within the hour tonight. I know this is the last thing you want to hear the night before a big meeting, and I take full responsibility for not catching it sooner. If it would help, I'm also happy to join the call early tomorrow to walk through exactly what changed, in case anyone asks. Please call me back as soon as you see this so we can make sure everything's accurate before the client sees it.", "tips": "For a workplace error, state clearly what went wrong and what you've already done to fix it, since managers want a solution alongside the bad news. Taking responsibility briefly, without over-explaining excuses, tends to sound more professional in this kind of message.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 54, "task_number": 6, "task_key": "difficult_situation", "task_title": "Dealing with a Difficult Situation", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "You promised to babysit your sister's kids on Saturday, but you've just been offered a job interview slot at the exact same time, one that took weeks to arrange. You need to call your sister to explain and work out a solution. Leave a voicemail explaining the situation.", "model_answer": "Hey, it's me. I know I promised I'd watch the kids Saturday afternoon, and I feel terrible even bringing this up, but something came up that I really can't move. The company I've been trying to get an interview with for weeks just offered me a slot Saturday at two, and it's honestly the only time they had available for the next month. I know how much you were counting on me, so I already reached out to Aunt Carla and she said she's free that afternoon if you'd like me to connect you with her instead. I could also take the kids the following weekend to make up for it, or any evening this week if that helps you get ahead on things. I'm really sorry for the short notice, especially since I know you probably had plans of your own for that time. Please call me back and let me know what works best, and thank you so much for understanding.", "tips": "When breaking a personal promise, acknowledge the impact on the other person before jumping to your own reason, since that shows you take the commitment seriously. Proposing a backup person or a makeup time, as this answer does, makes the request feel less one-sided.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 55, "task_number": 6, "task_key": "difficult_situation", "task_title": "Dealing with a Difficult Situation", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "You ordered a custom birthday cake for a family celebration, but when you picked it up, the bakery had spelled your father's name wrong and the event starts in three hours. You need to call the bakery to resolve this quickly. Leave a message explaining the situation.", "model_answer": "Hi, this is calling about the cake I just picked up for order number two-two-six. I got it home and noticed my dad's name is spelled wrong on the top, it says Fransis instead of Francis, and unfortunately this is for his birthday party starting in about three hours, so I don't have a lot of time to work with. I completely understand mistakes happen, and I'm not looking to cause trouble, I just really need this fixed quickly. Would it be possible for someone to redo just the piping on top while I wait, or if that's not doable, could I bring it back in for a quick fix within the next thirty minutes? I'd really appreciate a callback as soon as possible since the clock is ticking on this one. If neither of those options work, I'd also want to talk about a partial refund given the timing. Thanks so much, and I hope to hear back very soon.", "tips": "For time-sensitive service issues, state the deadline clearly and early so the listener understands the urgency without you having to repeat it. Present a preferred quick fix first, then a fallback option like a refund, so the message stays solution-focused rather than just complaining.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 56, "task_number": 6, "task_key": "difficult_situation", "task_title": "Dealing with a Difficult Situation", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Your neighbour's dog has been barking loudly every night this week, disturbing your sleep before an important week at work. You need to speak to your neighbour about this politely but firmly. Leave a note or voicemail explaining the situation.", "model_answer": "Hi, it's your neighbour from next door. I hope this doesn't come across the wrong way, I just wanted to mention something before it becomes a bigger issue between us. This past week, your dog has been barking pretty consistently around midnight, and it's been waking me up almost every night, which is rough timing since I've got a really demanding week at work coming up and need my sleep. I know dogs sometimes get anxious or restless, and I'm not trying to blame you at all, I just wanted to bring it to your attention in case you weren't aware it was happening that late. If there's anything that might help, like bringing him inside earlier or maybe a calming routine before bed, I'd really appreciate it. I know we've always gotten along well, and I just wanted to handle this directly with you rather than let it turn into a bigger annoyance. Thanks for hearing me out, and let me know if there's anything I can do to help too.", "tips": "When raising a sensitive complaint with someone you'll continue to see regularly, soften the delivery with goodwill statements while still stating the problem clearly, as this answer does. Suggesting a possible cause or solution, rather than only complaining, keeps the tone collaborative instead of confrontational.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 57, "task_number": 6, "task_key": "difficult_situation", "task_title": "Dealing with a Difficult Situation", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "You discover that a contractor you hired to renovate your kitchen has used a cheaper countertop material than the one specified in your signed contract. You need to call the contractor to address this. Leave a voicemail explaining the situation.", "model_answer": "Hi, it's calling about the kitchen renovation at my place. I was doing a final walkthrough today, and I noticed the countertop that was installed doesn't match what we agreed on in the contract. We specified the quartz composite finish, but what's installed looks like a laminate surface instead, and I want to make sure this gets addressed before I sign off on the final payment. I understand that sometimes there can be supply issues or mix-ups on a big job like this, so I'm not assuming anything was done deliberately, I just need to understand what happened and how we can fix it. I'd like the correct material installed as originally agreed, and if there's a delay involved in sourcing it, I'm willing to be reasonably flexible on timing as long as we're on the same page. Please give me a call back as soon as you can so we can go over the contract together and figure out next steps.", "tips": "For a contract dispute, reference the specific agreed detail explicitly, since vague complaints are easy to dismiss. Stay measured rather than accusatory, giving the other party a chance to explain, while still being clear about what outcome you expect.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 58, "task_number": 6, "task_key": "difficult_situation", "task_title": "Dealing with a Difficult Situation", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "You are sharing an apartment with a roommate who has consistently left dirty dishes in the sink for several days, and guests are coming over tomorrow. You need to raise this with your roommate. Leave a note or voicemail explaining the situation.", "model_answer": "Hey, it's me. I wanted to bring something up before it becomes a bigger deal, so it doesn't catch you off guard. The dishes have been piling up in the sink for about four or five days now, and honestly it's starting to bother me, especially since we've got people coming over tomorrow evening and I really don't want the kitchen looking like this when they arrive. I know things have probably just been busy on your end, so I'm not trying to make a big thing out of it, I just wanted to ask if we could both do a quick clean-up tonight so we're not scrambling tomorrow. Going forward, maybe we could try washing our own dishes within a day or two, just so it doesn't build up like this again. I really do appreciate everything else about living together, this is honestly the only thing I wanted to raise. Let me know if tonight works for you, or if there's a better time.", "tips": "Give a concrete, near-term reason for urgency, like guests arriving tomorrow, so the request feels timely rather than like a vague ongoing complaint. Ending on a positive note about the relationship, as this answer does, keeps the tone from feeling overly critical.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 59, "task_number": 6, "task_key": "difficult_situation", "task_title": "Dealing with a Difficult Situation", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "You were assigned to lead a group project at school, but one group member has missed every meeting so far and the deadline is in two days. You need to address this with that group member. Leave a voicemail explaining the situation.", "model_answer": "Hey, it's me from the group project. I wanted to check in because you've missed all three of our meetings so far, and with the deadline only two days away, I'm honestly starting to get a bit worried about how we're going to finish everything on time. I'm not calling to blame you, I know everyone gets busy, but I do need to know where things stand so I can plan the rest of our work around it. If there's something going on that's made it hard to join us, I completely understand, and we can figure out a smaller task you could handle on your own instead if meetings just aren't working right now. Otherwise, would you be able to join a quick call tonight so we can catch you up and split the remaining work fairly? I really want this to work out for everyone in the group, including you, so please call me back as soon as you get this.", "tips": "As a group leader, balance accountability with understanding by giving the person a chance to explain before assigning blame. Offer a concrete alternative, like a smaller individual task, so the message moves toward a solution rather than just expressing frustration.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 60, "task_number": 6, "task_key": "difficult_situation", "task_title": "Dealing with a Difficult Situation", "prep_seconds": 60, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "You realize you double-booked yourself, agreeing to attend a close friend's engagement party and a mandatory work training session on the same evening. You need to call your friend to explain and figure out a way to still be part of the celebration. Leave a voicemail explaining the situation.", "model_answer": "Hey, it's me, and I'm honestly a little embarrassed to be making this call. I just realized I double-booked myself for Friday, since work scheduled a mandatory training session that same evening, and I only just noticed the conflict with your engagement party. I feel awful about this because I know how important this celebration is to you, and I really don't want to miss it. I've already asked my manager if there's any flexibility, and worst case I might be able to slip out around eight and still catch the second half of the party if that's alright. If that doesn't work out, would it be okay if I came by beforehand for even twenty minutes to celebrate with you properly, or took you and your partner out for a dinner of your choosing this weekend instead? I really want to make sure you know how happy I am for you, even if the timing on my end is a mess right now.", "tips": "When a scheduling conflict is your own mistake, own it clearly rather than minimizing it, since that builds trust with the listener. Offering a partial presence, like arriving late, plus a backup plan shows genuine effort to make the celebration happen anyway.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 61, "task_number": 7, "task_key": "expressing_opinions", "task_title": "Expressing Opinions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Some people believe that remote work makes employees more productive, while others believe it makes them less connected and less accountable. What is your opinion? Give reasons and examples to support your view.", "model_answer": "In my opinion, remote work generally makes employees more productive, as long as the company sets clear expectations around communication and results. When I worked in an office, I noticed that a huge portion of my day disappeared into small interruptions, someone stopping by my desk to chat, unnecessary meetings that could have been a short message, or just the general noise of an open floor plan. Working from home cut a lot of that out, and I found I could actually finish focused tasks, like writing reports or reviewing budgets, in half the time it used to take me. That said, I do think the concern about connection is a fair one, and I don't want to dismiss it entirely. Teams that work remotely full time without any structure can start to feel disconnected, and it becomes harder to build the kind of trust that grows naturally from seeing someone every day. The way I see it, the solution isn't choosing one extreme or the other, but combining remote flexibility with occasional in-person check-ins, maybe once every couple of weeks, so people still build real relationships. Companies that manage this balance well tend to get the productivity benefits of remote work without losing the sense of team accountability that comes from regular contact. So overall, I lean toward remote work being the better model, provided it's managed thoughtfully rather than left completely unstructured.", "tips": "State your position clearly in the first sentence, then use a specific personal example to back it up, since concrete detail is more convincing than abstract claims. For a 90-second response, acknowledge the opposing view briefly before returning to and reinforcing your own opinion, which shows balanced thinking without weakening your stance.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 62, "task_number": 7, "task_key": "expressing_opinions", "task_title": "Expressing Opinions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Some people think schools should focus mainly on academic subjects like math and science, while others believe schools should spend equal time on practical life skills like budgeting and cooking. What is your opinion? Give reasons and examples to support your view.", "model_answer": "Personally, I believe schools should dedicate significantly more time to practical life skills than they currently do, even if that means slightly reducing the hours spent on some traditional academic subjects. Don't get me wrong, math and science are genuinely important, and I'm not suggesting we remove them from the curriculum. But I think back to my own graduation, and I realized I could solve a quadratic equation, yet I had absolutely no idea how to file taxes, negotiate a lease, or even plan a basic weekly grocery budget. Those gaps caused real stress in my early twenties, and I know several friends who went through the exact same thing, learning these skills the hard way through mistakes that cost them money. A single semester-long course covering budgeting, basic cooking, and understanding contracts would have saved a lot of us a great deal of trouble. Some people argue that these are things parents should teach at home, and in an ideal world maybe that's true, but not every family has the time, knowledge, or resources to cover all of it. Schools reach every student equally, regardless of their home situation, which makes them a fairer place to guarantee this kind of education. So while I value academics, I think a more balanced curriculum that treats life skills as equally essential would set young people up far better for actual adult life.", "tips": "Use a personal anecdote about a real gap in your own education to make an abstract opinion feel credible and specific. When addressing a counterargument like 'parents should teach this,' explain briefly why it doesn't fully solve the problem, which strengthens your position for the full 90 seconds.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 63, "task_number": 7, "task_key": "expressing_opinions", "task_title": "Expressing Opinions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Some people believe that social media has made society more connected, while others believe it has made people more isolated. What is your opinion? Give reasons and examples to support your view.", "model_answer": "I actually think social media has done both at the same time, but if I had to pick a side, I'd lean toward saying it has made people more isolated in the ways that matter most. It's true that platforms let me keep in touch with old classmates scattered across the country, and I do value seeing updates from relatives overseas that I'd otherwise lose touch with completely. But when I look at how people actually spend their time, I notice that scrolling through a feed often replaces the kind of deeper, sustained conversation that used to happen over a phone call or in person. I've caught myself liking a friend's post about a difficult week instead of actually calling to check on them, which feels like connection but really isn't the same thing at all. There's also research showing that heavy social media use correlates with higher loneliness among younger people, even with hundreds of online connections. What strikes me most is the quality difference, since a two-minute video call tends to leave me feeling more connected than an hour of scrolling through updates. So while social media gives the appearance of a wide network, I think it often substitutes shallow interaction for the deep connection that actually reduces isolation, which is why my opinion leans toward the negative side of this debate.", "tips": "When your opinion is genuinely mixed, it's fine to say so briefly, but still commit to a clear final stance so the response doesn't sound wishy-washy. Bringing in a general research point alongside personal experience adds credibility without requiring exact statistics.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 64, "task_number": 7, "task_key": "expressing_opinions", "task_title": "Expressing Opinions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Some people think it is better to live in a big city, while others prefer smaller towns. What is your opinion? Give reasons and examples to support your view.", "model_answer": "In my view, smaller towns generally offer a better overall quality of life, even though big cities certainly have their appeal. I lived in a large city for several years, and while I loved the variety of restaurants, the job opportunities, and the constant cultural events, I also noticed how much of my income disappeared into rent, and how much of my day got eaten up by long commutes on crowded transit. When I eventually moved to a smaller town, I found I could actually afford a place with a bit of outdoor space, and my commute shrank from nearly an hour each way down to about fifteen minutes, which gave me back a surprising amount of time and energy every single week. There's also something to be said for the sense of community in a smaller place, since I now recognize my neighbours, the local shop owners know my name, and that kind of familiarity was almost impossible to build in a city where everyone seemed to be constantly moving and rushing somewhere. I'll admit the trade-off is fewer job options and less nightlife, which matters more to some people, especially those earlier in their careers. But for me personally, and I suspect for a lot of people past their twenties, the calmer pace, lower cost, and stronger community make smaller towns the better choice overall.", "tips": "Structuring the response around a before-and-after comparison from your own experience, as this answer does with the move from city to town, gives the opinion a natural narrative shape that fills 90 seconds smoothly. Concede a genuine downside of your preferred option to sound balanced rather than one-sided.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 65, "task_number": 7, "task_key": "expressing_opinions", "task_title": "Expressing Opinions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Some people believe that competitive sports teach children valuable life lessons, while others think competition puts too much pressure on kids at a young age. What is your opinion? Give reasons and examples to support your view.", "model_answer": "I firmly believe that competitive sports, when introduced at the right age and with the right coaching approach, teach children lessons that are genuinely hard to replicate anywhere else. Growing up, I played on a soccer team that lost far more games than it won, and honestly, learning to handle that disappointment, shake hands with the other team, and show up again the following week taught me more about resilience than almost anything I learned in a classroom. Competition also introduces kids to the idea that effort doesn't always guarantee an immediate reward, which is an uncomfortable but important truth they'll encounter constantly as adults, whether in a job interview or a difficult project at work. That said, I do think the concern about pressure is legitimate, particularly when parents or coaches make winning the only measure of success, since I've seen kids as young as eight brought to tears over a lost game because the adults around them reacted so intensely. The difference, in my opinion, comes down entirely to how the adults frame competition, whether they celebrate effort and improvement or fixate purely on the scoreboard. A well-run youth league that emphasizes teamwork and growth over winning gives kids the benefits of competition without the damaging pressure, so the solution is better coaching, not removing competition altogether. Overall, I still land firmly on the side that competitive sports do more good than harm for most children.", "tips": "Use a childhood memory to illustrate an abstract life lesson, since specific sensory or emotional detail makes the opinion memorable rather than generic. Reframe the opposing concern as a solvable problem, as this answer does by blaming adult behaviour rather than competition itself, to keep your overall opinion intact.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 66, "task_number": 7, "task_key": "expressing_opinions", "task_title": "Expressing Opinions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Some people think governments should invest more heavily in public transit, while others believe money is better spent expanding and maintaining roads for cars. What is your opinion? Give reasons and examples to support your view.", "model_answer": "My opinion is that governments should prioritize public transit investment over road expansion, particularly in growing cities. I used to drive to work every day, and as the city grew, that twenty-minute commute gradually stretched to nearly fifty minutes because of increasing traffic, despite the city having widened the highway twice during that same period. What that experience taught me is that adding more lanes rarely solves congestion long term, since more road space just attracts more cars until it's congested again, a pattern that transportation researchers have documented repeatedly in cities around the world. When our city finally opened a new light rail line near my neighbourhood, I switched over almost immediately, and my commute dropped to about thirty minutes, with the added bonus that I could read or catch up on messages instead of sitting frustrated in traffic. There's also an environmental argument that I think deserves real weight, since a full transit train moves hundreds of people using a fraction of the emissions that the equivalent number of cars would produce. I understand the counterargument that not everyone lives near transit lines and that rural or spread-out areas genuinely need roads to function, and I don't dispute that at all. But for dense, growing urban areas specifically, I think the evidence strongly favours transit investment, both for reducing congestion and for the broader environmental and quality-of-life benefits it brings to residents.", "tips": "Bringing in a general, widely-known pattern (more lanes attracting more traffic) alongside your personal story gives the opinion more intellectual weight without needing exact citations. Narrow your claim to a specific context, such as dense cities, so the counterargument about rural areas doesn't undermine your overall position.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 67, "task_number": 7, "task_key": "expressing_opinions", "task_title": "Expressing Opinions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Some people believe that reading physical books is a better experience than reading on a screen, while others prefer the convenience of e-readers and tablets. What is your opinion? Give reasons and examples to support your view.", "model_answer": "I genuinely think this depends heavily on the type of reading involved, but for most everyday reading, I've come to prefer e-readers over physical books. I resisted this for a long time because I loved the feel and smell of a physical book, and there's still something nostalgic about browsing a bookstore shelf that a screen can't replicate. However, once I started travelling more for work, carrying even two or three physical books added noticeable weight and bulk to my bag, whereas an e-reader holds hundreds of titles and weighs about as much as a single paperback. I also found that the adjustable font size genuinely helped during a stretch when I was dealing with eye strain from long workdays, something a printed page simply can't accommodate. That said, I'll admit that for certain things, like a cookbook I want to flip through quickly, or a children's picture book I read with my niece, the physical format still wins by a wide margin, since screens can feel clunky for that kind of quick back-and-forth browsing. So my overall opinion is that convenience and practicality push me toward e-readers for novels and long-form reading, but I don't think physical books are becoming obsolete, they've just settled into the specific situations where the tactile experience genuinely adds value rather than just being sentimental preference.", "tips": "Distinguishing between different types of reading (novels versus cookbooks) lets you give a nuanced opinion that still lands on a clear overall preference, which works well for the longer 90-second format. Mentioning a specific personal circumstance, like eye strain, adds a reason beyond simple convenience.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 68, "task_number": 7, "task_key": "expressing_opinions", "task_title": "Expressing Opinions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Some people believe that university education should be free for everyone, while others believe students should continue to pay tuition. What is your opinion? Give reasons and examples to support your view.", "model_answer": "My opinion is that university education should be significantly more affordable than it currently is, even if making it entirely free everywhere isn't realistic given how governments fund public services. I graduated with a substantial amount of student debt, and I remember how that debt shaped nearly every major decision I made in my twenties, from delaying buying a car to putting off moving out on my own for an extra two years. Friends of mine who graduated with little to no debt, often because they came from families that could help, had a noticeably easier time starting their careers and taking reasonable financial risks, like accepting a lower-paying job in a field they were passionate about. That gap doesn't seem fair to me, since it means opportunity often depends more on family wealth than on ability or effort. At the same time, I recognize that completely free tuition for everyone, funded entirely by taxpayers, raises legitimate questions about fairness for people who never attend university and about where that significant funding would actually come from. So rather than entirely free tuition, I'd support a model with much lower baseline tuition combined with expanded needs-based grants, which would reduce the debt burden without placing the full cost on the general public. Overall, I believe the current system places too much financial risk on individual students, and meaningful reform is needed, even if free tuition isn't the exact solution.", "tips": "For a policy-style opinion question, it's fine to propose a middle-ground solution rather than picking an extreme side, as long as you state clearly what you actually believe should change. Personal financial impact stories make policy opinions feel grounded rather than abstract or textbook-like.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 69, "task_number": 7, "task_key": "expressing_opinions", "task_title": "Expressing Opinions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Some people think that artificial intelligence will create more jobs than it eliminates, while others believe it will lead to widespread job losses. What is your opinion? Give reasons and examples to support your view.", "model_answer": "I tend to believe that artificial intelligence will eliminate more jobs than it creates in the short term, even though I think new kinds of jobs will eventually emerge over a longer timeframe. In my own workplace, I've already seen a customer service team shrink after the company introduced an AI chatbot that handles the majority of routine inquiries, and the employees who remained had to shift into more specialized roles that not everyone was able to transition into successfully. That kind of disruption happens quickly, often within a year or two, while the new jobs that historically emerge from major technological shifts tend to take much longer to materialize and often require entirely different skill sets than the jobs that disappeared. I do think it's true that previous technological revolutions, like the introduction of computers into offices, eventually created more employment than they destroyed, and I'm not dismissing that historical pattern entirely. However, I think artificial intelligence is different in scale and speed, since it's capable of automating cognitive tasks across many industries simultaneously, rather than transforming one sector gradually over decades. Given how quickly this technology is advancing compared to how slowly retraining programs and education systems adapt, I think the near-term impact will be genuinely painful for a large number of workers, particularly in administrative and routine analytical roles, even if things eventually balance out generations from now.", "tips": "Distinguishing between short-term and long-term effects, as this answer does, is a strong technique for a nuanced opinion topic like technology's impact on jobs, since it lets you commit to a position without oversimplifying. A specific workplace example makes an otherwise abstract economic opinion feel concrete and personally observed.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 70, "task_number": 7, "task_key": "expressing_opinions", "task_title": "Expressing Opinions", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 90, "prompt_text": "Some people believe that it is better to specialize deeply in one skill or career, while others believe it is better to develop a broad range of skills across different areas. What is your opinion? Give reasons and examples to support your view.", "model_answer": "I lean toward believing that developing a broad range of skills tends to serve people better over the course of a full career, even though deep specialization clearly has its own advantages in certain fields. Early in my career, I focused entirely on one narrow technical skill, and while I became quite good at it, when that particular role became less in demand due to changes in the industry, I found myself struggling to pivot because I had almost no adjacent skills to fall back on. After that experience, I deliberately started picking up skills in project management, basic data analysis, and communication, areas somewhat outside my original specialty, and that combination ended up making me far more valuable and adaptable than being excellent at just one narrow thing. I recognize that certain professions, like surgery or highly advanced research, genuinely require deep specialization, and I wouldn't want a broadly-skilled generalist performing surgery on me, for instance. But for most careers, especially in a job market that keeps shifting due to automation and changing industry needs, I think the ability to adapt and combine different skills matters more than being narrowly excellent at just one thing. So overall, while I respect the value of deep expertise in specific fields, my personal experience has convinced me that breadth provides more security and opportunity for the majority of people over a full working life.", "tips": "Using your own career pivot as the central example gives this abstract opinion topic a clear narrative arc that naturally fills the full 90 seconds. Explicitly carving out an exception, like surgery requiring specialization, shows you've considered the topic from multiple angles rather than overgeneralizing.", "image": null, "image_2": null}, {"id": 71, "task_number": 8, "task_key": "unusual_situation", "task_title": "Describing an Unusual Situation", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: A man in full winter clothing, including a heavy parka, snow boots, and mittens, is sitting at an outdoor patio table in the middle of summer, with sunbathers in swimsuits nearby and a beach umbrella overhead, calmly sipping a hot drink from a thermos while everyone around him fans themselves in the heat. Describe what is happening in the picture and speculate about how this situation might have come about.", "model_answer": "In this picture, there's a man sitting at an outdoor patio table dressed head to toe in full winter gear, a heavy parka, snow boots, and mittens, even though it's clearly the middle of summer, since everyone around him is in swimwear, sunbathing under umbrellas in the heat. He's calmly sipping something from a thermos, looking completely unbothered by the temperature, which makes the scene pretty comical. My first guess is that he lost a bet with friends and agreed to wear full winter clothes in public for a day, and he's now stubbornly following through on it despite the heat. Another possibility is that he's filming a social media challenge or charity dare, testing how long he can last dressed like this. It could also be that he's trying out new cold-weather gear before a trip somewhere far colder and just picked an odd place to test it. Either way, he seems fully committed, sipping away like nothing unusual is happening.", "tips": "Describe the picture fully first, including the contrast between the person and their surroundings, before moving into speculation, since a rushed description makes the guessing feel disconnected. Offer two or three distinct, plausible explanations rather than settling on just one, which shows range and keeps the response filled for the full 60 seconds.", "image": "speaking_images/task8_71_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: A man in full winter clothing, including a heavy parka, snow boots, and mittens, is sitting at an outdoor patio table in the middle of summer, with sunbathers in swimsuits nearby and a beach umbrella overhead, calmly sipping a hot drink from a thermos while everyone around him fans themselves in the heat."}, {"id": 72, "task_number": 8, "task_key": "unusual_situation", "task_title": "Describing an Unusual Situation", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: A woman is walking six cats on individual leashes down a busy sidewalk, each cat wearing a tiny matching sweater, while pedestrians stop and take photos, and one cat is sitting stubbornly in the middle of the path refusing to move while the others continue trotting along calmly. Describe what is happening in the picture and speculate about how this situation might have come about.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a woman walking six cats at once down a busy sidewalk, each one on its own leash and wearing a matching little sweater, which already makes pedestrians stop and snap photos as she passes. What makes it funnier is that one cat has planted itself in the middle of the path and refuses to budge, while the other five just keep trotting along. My first thought is that she might run a small cat rescue and is walking her foster cats together to socialize them. It's also possible this is a promotional stunt for a pet clothing brand, since the sweaters look too coordinated to be a coincidence. Another option is that she's simply trained all six from kittens to walk on leashes, and today one of them just isn't in the mood, which explains the funny standoff while the others carry on without it.", "tips": "Point out the small comedic detail, like the one stubborn cat, since noticing specific quirks in the image shows careful observation rather than a generic summary. Ground your speculation in plausible real-world reasons, such as a business or hobby, rather than pure fantasy, which sounds more convincing.", "image": "speaking_images/task8_72_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: A woman is walking six cats on individual leashes down a busy sidewalk, each cat wearing a tiny matching sweater, while pedestrians stop and take photos, and one cat is sitting stubbornly in the middle of the path refusing to move while the others continue trotting along calmly."}, {"id": 73, "task_number": 8, "task_key": "unusual_situation", "task_title": "Describing an Unusual Situation", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: A delivery driver is standing at a customer's door holding a package, but instead of a house, the address leads to a small rowboat tied up at a dock on a lake, with a confused-looking man in the rowboat reaching up to sign for the delivery. Describe what is happening in the picture and speculate about how this situation might have come about.", "model_answer": "In this picture, a delivery driver is standing on a wooden dock holding a package, but instead of a house, the address seems to lead straight to a small rowboat tied up there, where a confused man is reaching up out of the boat to sign for it. Both of them look a little puzzled, which makes the scene pretty amusing. My best guess is that the man lives on a houseboat nearby and used the dock's general address when ordering online, so the app read it literally and sent the driver to the water instead of the houseboat further along the shore. Another possibility is he ordered fishing gear and requested delivery to wherever he happened to be that day, and the driver just went along with it since the coordinates matched exactly. It could also be a simple mix-up between a cottage address and a nearby boat rental office.", "tips": "When the odd scenario involves a specific process like delivery or an address, it helps to speculate about a system-level explanation, such as an app or address error, alongside a simpler personal one. Keep your tone light and observational, matching the humour of an unusual picture rather than treating it too seriously.", "image": "speaking_images/task8_73_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: A delivery driver is standing at a customer's door holding a package, but instead of a house, the address leads to a small rowboat tied up at a dock on a lake, with a confused-looking man in the rowboat reaching up to sign for the delivery."}, {"id": 74, "task_number": 8, "task_key": "unusual_situation", "task_title": "Describing an Unusual Situation", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: A man is mowing his lawn in the middle of a heavy snowstorm, pushing a regular gas lawnmower through several inches of snow while wearing a winter coat, and his next-door neighbour stands on their porch watching in obvious disbelief with a shovel still in hand. Describe what is happening in the picture and speculate about how this situation might have come about.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a man pushing an ordinary gas lawnmower through several inches of snow during a full snowstorm, bundled up in a winter coat, while his neighbour stands on the porch next door holding a shovel and staring at him in complete disbelief. It's a striking contrast, since one neighbour is dressed for shovelling while the other is mowing grass that isn't even visible under the snow. My first guess is that he simply mixed up his usual weekend chore routine on autopilot, grabbing the mower instead of the shovel without registering the weather. Another possibility is that this is some kind of dare with friends, where he agreed to mow regardless of conditions for a set stretch of time. It's also possible he's just testing whether the mower still runs after winter storage and picked an oddly comical moment to do it.", "tips": "Highlight the reaction of a secondary person in the picture, like the neighbour's disbelief, since it adds a second layer of detail beyond just describing the main action. Vary your speculation across habit, a bet, and a mundane practical reason to show flexible thinking within the 60 seconds.", "image": "speaking_images/task8_74_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: A man is mowing his lawn in the middle of a heavy snowstorm, pushing a regular gas lawnmower through several inches of snow while wearing a winter coat, and his next-door neighbour stands on their porch watching in obvious disbelief with a shovel still in hand."}, {"id": 75, "task_number": 8, "task_key": "unusual_situation", "task_title": "Describing an Unusual Situation", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: A woman is sitting in a crowded coffee shop with a full-sized parrot perched calmly on her shoulder, sipping from her cup, while several customers nearby lean away nervously and a barista peers over the counter with wide eyes, clearly unsure how to react. Describe what is happening in the picture and speculate about how this situation might have come about.", "model_answer": "In this picture, a woman is sitting in a busy coffee shop with a full-sized parrot perched right on her shoulder, completely at ease, even leaning down as if it's about to sip from her cup. Several customers nearby are visibly leaning away with nervous expressions, and the barista behind the counter is peeking over with wide eyes, unsure whether to say anything. My first guess is that the parrot is actually a registered emotional support animal, which would explain why she's allowed to bring it in despite the reactions around her. Another possibility is that she works nearby, perhaps at a pet store, and simply brought the bird along on a quick coffee run, treating it as completely normal. It could also be that this shop has some kind of pet-friendly policy regular customers already know about, while newer visitors simply aren't used to it yet.", "tips": "Note both the calm central figure and the nervous reactions of bystanders, since the contrast between the two is often what makes an unusual situation picture funny or striking. When speculating, consider whether there's a legitimate explanation, like a service animal, alongside more casual possibilities, to show balanced reasoning.", "image": "speaking_images/task8_75_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: A woman is sitting in a crowded coffee shop with a full-sized parrot perched calmly on her shoulder, sipping from her cup, while several customers nearby lean away nervously and a barista peers over the counter with wide eyes, clearly unsure how to react."}, {"id": 76, "task_number": 8, "task_key": "unusual_situation", "task_title": "Describing an Unusual Situation", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: At a formal wedding reception, the groom is seen fully dressed in his tuxedo but wearing bright yellow rubber rain boots instead of dress shoes, dancing energetically in the middle of the dance floor while guests in elegant attire watch and laugh, and the bride is doubled over laughing beside him. Describe what is happening in the picture and speculate about how this situation might have come about.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a wedding reception where the groom is dressed in a sharp formal tuxedo, but instead of dress shoes, he's wearing bright yellow rubber rain boots, dancing energetically on the dance floor. The other guests, in elegant attire, are watching and laughing, and the bride herself is doubled over laughing right beside him, so nobody seems bothered at all. My first guess is that his actual dress shoes were lost or forgotten, maybe left behind at the hotel, and the rain boots were the only backup available, so he decided to embrace it rather than stress. Another possibility is that it rained during the outdoor ceremony earlier, so he switched into boots to protect his real shoes and simply never changed back. It could also be a planned running joke he and the bride arranged beforehand for memorable photos.", "tips": "For a wedding or formal-event scenario, note the mismatch between the setting's formality and the odd detail, since that contrast is usually the whole point of the humour. Considering both an accidental cause (lost shoes) and a deliberate one (planned joke) covers a good range of speculation for the response.", "image": "speaking_images/task8_76_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: At a formal wedding reception, the groom is seen fully dressed in his tuxedo but wearing bright yellow rubber rain boots instead of dress shoes, dancing energetically in the middle of the dance floor while guests in elegant attire watch and laugh, and the bride is doubled over laughing beside him."}, {"id": 77, "task_number": 8, "task_key": "unusual_situation", "task_title": "Describing an Unusual Situation", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: A man is standing in line at a busy grocery store checkout wearing a full scuba diving suit, complete with a mask pushed up on his forehead and flippers on his feet, calmly holding a basket with just a carton of milk and a loaf of bread, while the cashier and other shoppers stare in confusion. Describe what is happening in the picture and speculate about how this situation might have come about.", "model_answer": "In this picture, a man is standing in line at a busy grocery store checkout dressed in a full scuba diving suit, flippers and all, with his mask pushed up on his forehead, holding a small basket with just a carton of milk and a loaf of bread. The cashier and nearby shoppers are clearly staring in confusion, since a scuba outfit is about as far from typical grocery attire as it gets. My first guess is that he's on his way to or from a diving lesson and simply stopped in for a couple of essentials rather than changing out of his gear first. Another possibility is that he's doing a charity challenge, agreeing to wear the suit in public for a whole day to raise money. It's also possible the flippers made walking so awkward that he wanted to grab just two items and get out quickly.", "tips": "Explain why the basket contains so few items, since noticing small logical details like this shows attentive description rather than just naming the obvious costume. Suggesting a practical reason (a diving lesson) alongside a more unusual one (a charity dare) gives your speculation good variety.", "image": "speaking_images/task8_77_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: A man is standing in line at a busy grocery store checkout wearing a full scuba diving suit, complete with a mask pushed up on his forehead and flippers on his feet, calmly holding a basket with just a carton of milk and a loaf of bread, while the cashier and other shoppers stare in confusion."}, {"id": 78, "task_number": 8, "task_key": "unusual_situation", "task_title": "Describing an Unusual Situation", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: In a quiet public library, a man is standing on top of a study table playing an accordion loudly, while nearby students with headphones on continue studying completely undisturbed, and a librarian approaches with a stern expression and a raised finger to her lips. Describe what is happening in the picture and speculate about how this situation might have come about.", "model_answer": "This picture shows a man standing right on top of a study table inside a quiet public library, playing an accordion loudly, which is about as far from typical library behaviour as you can get. The students nearby seem unbothered, mostly because they've got headphones on, while a librarian approaches with a stern expression and a finger raised to her lips. My first thought is that he's rehearsing for a busking audition later that day and mistakenly assumed the library would be an acceptable quiet spot, not realizing how disruptive an accordion is. Another possibility is that this is a planned video for some kind of challenge where people try to get away with odd behaviour, which would explain the librarian's annoyed reaction. It's also possible he simply misunderstood the library's rules, thinking a specific room allowed practice sessions when it didn't.", "tips": "Use the contrast between the disruptive action and the calm students in headphones to add humour and specificity to your description before moving into speculation. Referencing a modern context like a video challenge alongside more ordinary explanations, like a misunderstanding, gives your guesses good range.", "image": "speaking_images/task8_78_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: In a quiet public library, a man is standing on top of a study table playing an accordion loudly, while nearby students with headphones on continue studying completely undisturbed, and a librarian approaches with a stern expression and a raised finger to her lips."}, {"id": 79, "task_number": 8, "task_key": "unusual_situation", "task_title": "Describing an Unusual Situation", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: A man is sitting in the waiting room of a dentist's office wearing a full suit of medieval-style armor, helmet included, calmly reading a magazine while the receptionist behind the desk stares at him with her mouth open and another patient nearby quietly takes a photo on their phone. Describe what is happening in the picture and speculate about how this situation might have come about.", "model_answer": "In this picture, a man is sitting calmly in a dentist's office waiting room dressed in a full suit of medieval-style armour, helmet and all, casually flipping through a magazine as if nothing is unusual. The receptionist is staring at him with her mouth wide open, and another patient nearby is quietly taking a photo, clearly finding the scene just as strange. My first guess is that he's part of a reenactment group or fencing club, and his appointment fell right after a rehearsal, so he just kept the armour on for convenience. Another possibility is that he works as a performer at a themed restaurant nearby and his appointment was scheduled during a break in his costume shift. It's also possible this is a lighthearted bet where he agreed to wear the armour through a list of everyday errands, and the dentist was simply one of the stops.", "tips": "For costume-based unusual situations, consider whether the person might have come from or be heading to a related activity, like a reenactment or performance, since that's often the most natural explanation. Note the bystanders' specific reactions, like the photo being taken, to make your description feel vivid rather than a flat summary.", "image": "speaking_images/task8_79_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: A man is sitting in the waiting room of a dentist's office wearing a full suit of medieval-style armor, helmet included, calmly reading a magazine while the receptionist behind the desk stares at him with her mouth open and another patient nearby quietly takes a photo on their phone."}, {"id": 80, "task_number": 8, "task_key": "unusual_situation", "task_title": "Describing an Unusual Situation", "prep_seconds": 30, "response_seconds": 60, "prompt_text": "Picture: On a city street, a man is riding a unicycle while carrying a tall stack of pizza boxes balanced on one hand, weaving smoothly between pedestrians, while a restaurant owner stands outside his shop with his arms crossed shaking his head, and a curious dog on a leash tries to jump up at the boxes as they pass. Describe what is happening in the picture and speculate about how this situation might have come about.", "model_answer": "In this picture, a man is riding a unicycle down a busy city street while balancing a tall stack of pizza boxes on one hand, weaving smoothly between pedestrians as if he's done this a hundred times before. Outside a nearby shop, a restaurant owner stands with his arms crossed shaking his head, while a curious dog on a leash tries to jump up toward the boxes as they pass. My first guess is that he's a delivery driver for a pizza shop using a unicycle as a quirky gimmick to stand out and generate attention around town. The restaurant owner's frustration might come from being a nearby competitor annoyed that the gimmick is drawing customers away from his own shop. Another possibility is that this is a personal bet with friends, seeing if he could deliver pizzas without dropping them while riding a unicycle, which also explains the dog reacting to the smell.", "tips": "When two odd details appear together, like the unicycle and the disapproving shop owner, try connecting them into one coherent story, such as a marketing gimmick and a rival business, rather than treating them as unrelated facts. Mentioning the small background detail of the dog shows thorough observation and helps fill the response naturally.", "image": "speaking_images/task8_80_a.jpg", "image_2": null, "image_description": "Picture: On a city street, a man is riding a unicycle while carrying a tall stack of pizza boxes balanced on one hand, weaving smoothly between pedestrians, while a restaurant owner stands outside his shop with his arms crossed shaking his head, and a curious dog on a leash tries to jump up at the boxes as they pass."}];
